text:
The credit rating of ReStore Retail Group Limited (hereinafter, Inventive Retail Group, or the Company) and its outlook  have been affirmed due to the continuation of the gradual recovery of Inventive Retail Group’s business after a period of significant uncertainty in 2022. The credit rating continues to reflect the medium score for market position, strong business profile, and very strong geographic diversification. The financial risk profile assessment is based on the medium size of the Company, and medium assessments of leverage, coverage and cash flow. The rating is supported by high profitability.
Inventive Retail Group is a group of companies that owns a chain of stores specializing in selling the products of Apple (re:Store project), Samsung, Xiaomi, and other well-known brands. The Company also promotes multi-brand formats such as Street Beat, Amazing Red, and Mir Kubikov (World of Bricks). The Company carries out direct corporate sales and implements e-commerce projects for various brands. The Group’s chain included more than 450 stores as of the end of May 2024.
KEY ASSESSMENT FACTORS
Strong business profile. Inventive Retail Group managed to overcome the period of uncertainty and cope with the risks that arose in 2022, which allowed the Agency to predict the recovery of the Company’s business in 2023. Last year’s results are evidence of a stable recovery trend — the Company increased revenues in key business segments (consumer electronics and sportswear), including thanks to the opening of a chain of sportswear stores, Amazing Red, demonstrating demand for the multi-brand format. The Company continues to work on optimizing its store network and assortment, and is also developing work with marketplaces as a sales channel.
High assessment of profitability and medium size of business. In 2023, the Company’s revenues grew by 12% year-on-year (to RUB 66 bln), in line with the Agency’s forecast. In 2024, ACRA assumes that revenues will grow by up to 25%, and then slow in 2025–2026 due to the transition from recovery growth to organic growth. The weighted average FFO indicator before fixed payments and taxes for 2021 to 2026 is expected to be RUB 10.9 bln.
The FFO margin before fixed payments and taxes, which reached 20% in 2022, returned to the values of 2021 and amounted to 12% as of the end of 2023. The Agency does not expect profitability to fall below 10% in 2024–2026.
Medium assessments of leverage and coverage. The Company increased its debt in 2023, which was due to business conditions that changed in 2022 (in particular, a change in the procedure for settlements with suppliers). Starting in 2024, the Agency assumes that Inventive Retail Group’s need for borrowed funds will begin to decline on the back of optimized supply conditions. ACRA estimates that weighted average lease-adjusted total debt to FFO before fixed payments will be 4.3x, and the ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments to be 1.7x averaged for 2021–2026.
Higher debt and interest rates put pressure on the debt service assessment. The ratio of FFO before net interest payments to interest payments declined considerably in 2023 (to 2.9x), while the ratio of FFO before fixed payments to fixed payments declined more gradually (to 1.4x). The Agency expects these indicators to grow as debt declines, the Company’s business recovers, and rates fall over the medium term.
Strong liquidity and medium cash flow assessment. In June 2024, the Company repaid a bond issue of RUB 3 bln, and thereby decided to not refinance debt by issuing bonds, instead giving preference to bank lending and digital financial assets (the Company conducted two placements of these assets to a total of RUB 1.4 bln in 2024). Inventive Retail Group usually maintains significant volumes of available credit lines, which is positively assessed by the Agency.
ACRA has improved the assessment of the Company’s cash flow to medium and expects free cash flow (FCF) to return to positive values from 2024, given the recovery of business growth, low level of capital expenditures, and balanced approach to dividend payments.
KEY ASSUMPTIONS
The Company maintaining its ability to import products without significant negative changes to conditions;
Revenues growing by 25% in 2024 and 6–7% in 2025–2026;
FFO margin before fixed payments and taxes at 13–14% in 2023–2025;
No dividend payments in 2024;
The share of capital expenditures at 1–2% of revenues in 2024–2026;
Continued access to external liquidity sources.
POTENTIAL OUTLOOK OR RATING CHANGE FACTORS
The Stable outlook assumes that the rating will highly likely stay unchanged within the 12 to 18-month horizon.
A positive rating action may be prompted by:
The weighted average ratio of adjusted total debt to FFO before fixed payments declining below 1.0x and weighted average ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments declining below 1.0x;
The weighted average ratio of FFO before fixed payments to fixed payments exceeding 5.0x;
The weighted average FCF margin exceeding 2%;
Substantial improvement of corporate governance practices.
A negative rating action may be prompted by:
FFO before fixed payments and taxes declining below RUB 5 bln;
FFO margin before fixed payments and taxes falling below 5%;
Ratio of adjusted total debt to FFO before fixed payments exceeding 6.0x;
Ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments growing above 5.0x;
Ratio of FFO before fixed payments to fixed payments falling below 1.5x;
Consistently negative FCF margin in 2024–2026;
Deterioration of access to external liquidity sources.
RATING COMPONENTS
Standalone creditworthiness assessment (SCA): a-.
Support: none.
ISSUE RATINGS
No outstanding issues have been rated.
REGULATORY DISCLOSURE
The credit rating has been assigned to ReStore Retail Group Limited under the national scale for the Russian Federation based on the Methodology for Credit Ratings Assignment to Non-Financial Corporations under the National Scale for the Russian Federation and the Key Concepts Used by the Analytical Credit Rating Agency within the Scope of Its Rating Activities.
The credit rating of ReStore Retail Group Limited was published by ACRA for the first time on February 20, 2021. The credit rating and its outlook are expected to be revised within one year following the publication date of this press release.
The credit rating was assigned based on data provided by ReStore Retail Group Limited, information from publicly available sources, and ACRA’s own databases. The credit rating is solicited and the ReStore Retail Group participated in its assignment.
In assigning the credit rating, ACRA used only information, the quality and reliability of which were, in ACRA’s opinion, appropriate and sufficient to apply the methodologies.
ACRA provided no additional services to ReStore Retail Group. No conflicts of interest were discovered in the course of credit rating assignment.

The credit rating of PJSC “MegaFon” (hereinafter, MegaFon, or the Company) has been upgraded due to the improvement of the Company’s business profile thanks to stronger financial and operational results for 2022 in its key business segment (mobile communications) compared to its main competitors, coupled with a better qualitative assessment of leverage. The financial risk profile continues to be characterized by very high profitability, a medium cash flow, which is adversely impacted by dividend payments, as well as strong liquidity, and medium leverage and debt servicing.
MegaFon is one of the largest telecommunications companies in Russia, providing mobile and fixed line communication services, mobile and broadband internet access, digital television, and solutions in the area of big data analysis and processing, cybersecurity, etc.
KEY ASSESSMENT FACTORS
Very strong operational risk profile. As of the end of 2022, the Company’s network included around 210,000 base stations and the length of FOCL was 223,700 km. The Company’s revenues are stable across several business segments, yet the leading segment with a share of around 75% of revenues (around 80% in 2021) continues to be mobile communications services. ACRA does not expect the revenue structure for provided services to change in the forecast period.
At the end of 2021, First Tower Company JSC consolidated the Company’s tower infrastructure, however, taking into account MegaFon’s equity share in First Tower Company JSC and the presence of long-term infrastructure lease agreements, the Infrastructure Ownership Model sub-factor is assessed as very high.
Very high assessments of profitability and business size. Weighted average FFO before fixed payments and taxes for 2021–2026 amounts to RUB 169.3 bln, which according to ACRA’s methodology corresponds to a very large size of business. The number of users in Russia grew to 75.3 mln last year (vs. 74.4 mln in 2021). The weighted average FFO margin before fixed payments and taxes is 39% for 2021–2026.
Medium leverage and debt servicing. The Company’s loan portfolio continues to be mainly ruble-denominated credit lines and is well diversified by lenders. The weighted average ratio of lease-adjusted total debt to FFO before fixed payments for 2021–2026 is 2.6x, while the weighted average ratio of FFO before fixed payments to fixed payments for the same period is 3.7x.
Medium cash flow and strong liquidity. The share of CAPEX in revenues remains at the same level as in previous years. ACRA expects it to remain at 14–16% over the forecast horizon of 2023–2026. Dividend payments put certain pressure on the assessment of the FCF margin. Given the presence of significant undrawn credit lines and the high qualitative assessment of liquidity, the overall assessment of the Company’s liquidity factor is strong.
KEY ASSUMPTIONS
Average annual revenue growth rate at 3–5% in 2023–2025;
User base stability, with average growth of ARPU (average revenue per user) at 2–3% per year;
Capital expenditures and dividend payments over the forecast horizon according to the presented financial model;
Maintaining a significant volume of undrawn credit lines.
POTENTIAL OUTLOOK OR RATING CHANGE FACTORS
The Stable outlook assumes that the rating will highly likely stay unchanged within the 12 to 18-month horizon.
A negative rating action may be prompted by:
Weighted average FFO before fixed payments and taxes falling below RUB 100 bln;
FFO margin before fixed payments and taxes falling below 40% over the forecast horizon;
Weighted average ratio of lease-adjusted total debt to FFO before fixed payments exceeding 3.5х;
Weighted average ratio of FFO before fixed payments to fixed payments falling below 2.5x;
Weighted average FCF margin falling below 2%;
Weighted average ratio of capital expenditures to revenues exceeding 20%;
Deterioration in the liquidity profile;
Significant payment of dividends over the forecast horizon;
Deteriorating corporate governance practices (including information transparency).
RATING COMPONENTS
Standalone creditworthiness assessment (SCA): aaa.
Support: none.
ISSUE RATINGS
PJSC “MegaFon”, series BO-002P-02 (RU000A1009A1), maturity date: April 6, 2026, issue volume: RUB 10 bln — AAA(RU);
PJSC “MegaFon”, series BO-002P-01 (RU000A1006H2), maturity date: March 11, 2024, issue volume: RUB 5 bln — AAA(RU);
PJSC “MegaFon”, series BO-001P-06 (RU000A1006G4), maturity date: March 11, 2024, issue volume: RUB 5 bln — AAA(RU);
PJSC “MegaFon”, series BO-002P-03 (RU000A1065S5), maturity date: April 23, 2025, issue volume: RUB 20 bln — AAA(RU).
Rationale. The issues above represent senior unsecured debt of MegaFon. Due to the absence of either structural or contractual subordination of the issues, the Agency regards them as equal to other existing and future unsecured and unsubordinated debt obligations of the Company in terms of priority. According to ACRA’s methodology, taking into account the very high level of the Company’s creditworthiness, the Agency applied the simplified approach, according to which the credit ratings of the issues are equivalent to that of MegaFon, i.e. AAA(RU).
REGULATORY DISCLOSURE
The credit ratings have been assigned to PJSC “MegaFon” and the bond issues (RU000A1009A1, RU000A1006H2, RU000A1006G4, RU000A1065S5) of PJSC “MegaFon” under the national scale for the Russian Federation based on the Methodology for Credit Ratings Assignment to Non-Financial Corporations under the National Scale for the Russian Federation and the Key Concepts Used by the Analytical Credit Rating Agency Within the Scope of Its Rating Activities. The Methodology for Assigning Credit Ratings to Financial Instruments under the National Scale for the Russian Federation was also applied to assign credit ratings to the above issues.
The credit ratings of PJSC “MegaFon” and the bond issues (RU000A1009A1, RU000A1006H2, RU000A1006G4, RU000A1065S5) of PJSC “MegaFon” were published by ACRA for the first time on September 21, 2017, April 15, 2019, March 18, 2019, March 18, 2019, and April 26, 2023, respectively. The credit rating of PJSC “MegaFon” and its outlook and the credit ratings of the bond issues (RU000A1009A1, RU000A1006H2, RU000A1006G4, RU000A1065S5) of PJSC “MegaFon” are expected to be revised within one year following the publication date of this press release.
The credit ratings were assigned based on data provided by PJSC “MegaFon”, information from publicly available sources, and ACRA’s own databases. The rating analysis was performed using the IFRS financial statements of PJSC “MegaFon” for 2022. The credit ratings are solicited and PJSC “MegaFon” participated in their assignment.
Disclosure of deviations from approved methodologies: given the existence of long-term lease agreements for communications tower infrastructure, the Infrastructure Ownership Model sub-factor was assessed with a deviation from the Methodology for Credit Ratings Assignment to Non-Financial Corporations under the National Scale for the Russian Federation.
In assigning the credit ratings, ACRA used only information, the quality and reliability of which were, in ACRA’s opinion, appropriate and sufficient to apply the methodologies.
ACRA provided additional services to PJSC “MegaFon”. No conflicts of interest were discovered in the course of credit rating assignment.

The credit rating of Rissa Investments Limited (hereinafter, the Company, or the Group) has been affirmed due to the unblocking of the bank accounts of IDS Borjomi Georgia LLC and the recovery of operations of the Group’s Georgian segment, as well as the release of 2021 consolidated financial reporting that corresponds to the Agency’s initial assessments. The Developing outlook has been retained mainly due to the uncertain prospects of the Ukrainian market, which, according to ACRA, accounted for 37% of the Company’s revenues in 2021.
ACRA retains its medium assessment of the Company’s business profile and continues to highly rate its market position, which is largely due to the presence of well-known brands in the Group’s portfolio. The Group’s financial risk profile continues to be characterized by a medium assessment of leverage, high profitability, and negative free cash flow (FCF).
Rissa Investments Limited is a holding company that consolidates bottled water production assets in Russia, Georgia, and Ukraine. The portfolio of the Company’s brands includes Borjomi, Saint Spring, and others.
KEY ASSESSMENT FACTORS
Recovery of operations of the Georgian segment. In April 2022, information emerged stating that the Company had decided to suspend the operations of its Borjomi plants due to difficulties encountered by IDS Borjomi Georgia LLC in receiving foreign currency revenues. According to the Group, its foreign currency accounts were unblocked by Georgian banks following the key shareholder’s decision to transfer part of its stake to the Georgian Government and reduce its share in the Company to below 50%. This allowed the Georgian segment to resume operations. ACRA positively views the resumption of production at the Borjomi plants. However, the Developing outlook is still in place due to the uncertainty surrounding the Ukrainian market.
Leverage is still assessed as medium. The structure of the Group’s debt continues to be stable — the basis of the debt portfolio is a bond issue, as well as loans from Sberbank (ACRA rating AAA(RU), outlook Stable) and JSC “ALFA-BANK” (ACRA rating AA+, outlook Positive). According to the Group’s audited consolidated financial statements, the ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments was 2.1x in 2021 and the ratio of FFO before net interest payments to interest payments was 7.2x, which corresponds to medium leverage. ACRA maintains its forecast for 2022 in the range of 2.0–2.5x and 3.0–4.0x, respectively.
The high liquidity assessment has been maintained due to the Group’s sufficiently comfortable debt repayment schedule — major repayment of debt is expected in 2024, during which a put option for the bond issue is to be exercised. The rest of the debt is repaid relatively evenly. The presence of undrawn credit lines also positively affects the assessment of this factor. The main pressure on liquidity, according to ACRA, may come from FCF, which, according to the Agency’s forecast, may be negative in 2022.
KEY ASSUMPTIONS
Maintaining production volumes of the Georgian segment in 2022 at the 2021 level;
Maintaining production volumes of the Russian segment in 2022 at the 2021 level;
Production volumes of the Ukrainian segment falling by 10–20% in 2022 compared to 2021;
Capital expenditures program reduced to 10–20% of revenues in 2022–2024;
No dividend payments until complete recovery of all the Group’s sales markets;
Access to external liquidity sources.
POTENTIAL OUTLOOK OR RATING CHANGE FACTORS
The Developing outlook indicates a variety of trends: the rating may stay unchanged, be upgraded or downgraded.
A positive rating action may be prompted by:
Weighted average ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments declining below 2.0x;
Weighted average ratio of FFO before net interest payments to interest payments growing above 5.0х;
Positive FCF profitability.
A negative rating action may be prompted by:
Weighted average ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments growing above 3.5x;
Weighted average ratio of FFO before net interest payments to interest payments declining below 2.5х;
FFO profitability before fixed payments and taxes falling below 12%;
Any dividend payments before the complete recovery of all the Group’s sales markets;
Changes to the structure and membership of the Group which may lead to a deterioration of financial performance and infringement of creditors’ rights;
Worsened access to external liquidity sources.
RATING COMPONENTS
Standalone creditworthiness assessment (SCA): a-.
Support: none.
ISSUE RATINGS
Bond issued by Borjomi Finance LLC, series 001P-01 (RU000A102SK1), maturity date: February 20, 2026, issue volume: RUB 7 bln — A-(RU).
Rationale. The issue represents senior unsecured debt of Borjomi Finance LLC, a subsidiary of Rissa Investments Limited. The credit rating is based on a guarantee from Rissa Investments Limited, public irrevocable offers from the Group’s trading companies (IDS Borjomi Beverages Co. N. V., Curacao, and LLC “IDS Borjomi (Moscow)”), its plants in Russia (LLC “Aqua Star” (Kostroma) and LLC “Edelweiss L” (Lipetsk)), and Georgia-based IDS Borjomi Georgia LLC. Despite the existence of suretyships from the key operating companies of Rissa Investments Limited, ACRA applied the detailed approach to assess the reimbursement rate due to a significant amount of secured debt and a noticeable time difference between the dates of bond purchases for different groups of offerors. According to this approach and the Agency’s methodology, the recovery rate for the issue is category II, and therefore the credit rating of the issue is equal to the credit rating of Rissa Investments Limited, i.e. A-(RU).
REGULATORY DISCLOSURE
The credit ratings of Rissa Investments Limited and the bond (RU000A102SK1) issued by Borjomi Finance LLC, a subsidiary of Rissa Investments Limited, have been assigned under the national scale for the Russian Federation based on the Methodology for Credit Ratings Assignment to Non-Financial Corporations under the National Scale for the Russian Federation and the Key Concepts Used by the Analytical Credit Rating Agency within the Scope of Its Rating Activities. The Methodology for Assigning Credit Ratings to Individual Issues of Financial Instruments on the National Scale for the Russian Federation was also applied to assign the credit rating to the above issue.
The credit ratings of Rissa Investments Limited and the bond (RU000A102SK1) issued by Borjomi Finance LLC were published by ACRA for the first time on May 21, 2020 and February 26, 2021, respectively. The credit rating of Rissa Investments Limited and its outlook and the credit rating of the bond (RU000A102SK1) issued by Borjomi Finance LLC are expected to be revised within one year following the publication date of this press release.
The credit ratings were assigned based on data provided by Rissa Investments Limited, information from publicly available sources, and ACRA’s own databases. The credit ratings are solicited, and Rissa Investments Limited participated in their assignment.
Deviations from approved methodologies: the Geographical Diversification factor was assessed with a deviation from the Methodology for Credit Ratings Assignment to Non-Financial Corporations under the National Scale for the Russian Federation taking into account the geography of the Company’s operations and its key sales markets.
In assigning the credit ratings, ACRA used only information, the quality and reliability of which was, in ACRA’s opinion, appropriate and sufficient to apply the methodologies.
ACRA provided no additional services to Rissa Investments Limited and Borjomi Finance LLC. No conflicts of interest were discovered in the course of credit rating assignment.

The outlook on the credit rating of Rissa Investments Limited (hereinafter, the Company, or the Group) has been changed due to the unfavorable situation in the Company’s key sales markets, as well as the problems faced by IDS Borjomi Georgia LLC with receiving foreign currency revenues, which have led to the Group suspending the operations of its Borjomi plants. Sales of Borjomi accounted for around 40% of the Group’s revenues and therefore a long suspension of production of this brand may result in growth of consolidated leverage.
ACRA retains its average assessment of the Company’s business profile and continues to highly rate its market position, which is largely due to the presence of well-known brands in the Group’s portfolio. Despite falling, capital expenditures continue to result in a negative free cash flow (FCF).
Rissa Investments Limited is a holding company that consolidates bottled water production assets in Russia, Georgia, and Ukraine. The portfolio of the Company’s brands includes Borjomi, Saint Spring, and others.
KEY ASSESSMENT FACTORS
Falling revenues in 2022 and an uncertain forecast for 2023–2024. In 2021, the Group’s revenues grew to RUB 41 bln (+24%). Sales of Borjomi accounted for around 40% of the Group’s consolidated revenues, while sales of local Russian and Ukrainian brands accounted for around 25% each. However, in 2022, the Company has faced difficulties in selling its Ukrainian segment products, as well as Borjomi. ACRA assumes that in the event of a relatively quickly resolution of the difficulties facing IDS Borjomi Georgia LLC, the Company may be able to avoid falling sales by volume thanks to a sufficient supply of ready products. The Agency assesses the possible decline of sales of Ukrainian local brands at 10–20%, while the performance of the Russian segment by volume, in ACRA’s opinion, will be similar to 2021. The Agency’s base case scenario foresees revenues at around the 2021 level as prices grow due to inflation. Future results will primarily depend on geopolitical factors and sanctions.
ACRA assesses the Company’s FFO before net interest payments and taxes in 2021 at RUB 8 bln, which continues to correspond to a medium assessment of business size as per ACRA’s classification. FFO profitability before net interest payments and taxes stood at 19% in 2021. ACRA assumes that FFO before net interest payments and taxes may fall by 3–5% in 2022 while maintaining the level of profitability.
Higher leverage. The Group’s debt as of December 31, 2021 was around RUB 16 bln. In 2021, the Company placed ruble-denominated bonds, having refinanced the foreign currency loans of the Georgian segment, and thereby brought the foreign currency debt structure into line with the revenue structure. Besides the bond issue, which amounts to 42% of the debt portfolio, loans from Sberbank (ACRA rating AAA(RU), outlook Stable) and JSC “ALFA-BANK” (ACRA rating AA+(RU), outlook Positive) account for significant shares of the portfolio — 35% and 11%, respectively.
The Group’s total ratio of debt to FFO before net interest payments for 2021 increased from 1.7x to 2.2x, which corresponds to medium leverage. This is mainly due to the financing of the capital expenditure program. ACRA assumes that this indicator will be within the range of 2.0–2.5x for 2022. As leverage and interest rates grow (most of the debt financing was raised at floating rates), interest payment coverage is declining — the ratio of FFO before net interest payments to interest payments fell to 6.2x in 2021, and ACRA expects that it may approach 3.0x in 2022.
Significant pressure on liquidity due to anticipated negative FCF. FCF was negative in 2021 due to a peak in capital expenditures (ratio of CAPEX to revenues at 24% in 2021) and payment of dividends. In 2022, the Group plans to suspend dividend payments and substantially curtail its capital expenditure program, which should provide considerable support to FCF, which, according to ACRA’s assessments continues to be negative this year. This in turn will put considerable pressure on liquidity. The unfavorable macroeconomic situation will negatively impact the Group’s access to liquidity sources. At the same time, the Company has stated the availability of around RUB 2 bln in undrawn credit lines. Major repayment of debt is expected in 2024, during which a put option for the bond issue is to be exercised. The rest of the debt is repaid relatively evenly.
KEY ASSUMPTIONS
Maintaining production volumes of the Georgian segment in 2022 at the 2021 level and a rather fast restart of operations at the Borjomi plants;
Maintaining production volumes of the Russian segment in 2022 at the 2021 level;
Production volumes of the Ukrainian segment falling by 10–20% in 2022 compared to 2021;
Capital expenditures program reduced to 10–20% of revenues in 2022–2024;
No dividend payments until complete recovery of all the Group’s sales markets;
Access to external liquidity sources.
POTENTIAL OUTLOOK OR RATING CHANGE FACTORS
The Developing outlook indicates a variety of trends: the rating may stay unchanged, be upgraded or downgraded.
A positive rating action may be prompted by:
Full resumption of operations at all the Group’s plants;
Weighted average ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments declining below 2.0x;
Weighted average ratio of FFO before net interest payments to interest payments growing above 5.0х;
Positive FCF profitability.
A negative rating action may be prompted by:
Operation of Borjomi plants suspended for an extensive period of time;
Weighted average ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments growing above 3.5x;
Weighted average ratio of FFO before net interest payments to interest payments declining below 2.5х;
FFO profitability before fixed payments and taxes falling below 12%;
Any dividend payments before the complete recovery of all the Group’s sales markets;
Changes to the structure and membership of the Group which may lead to a deterioration of financial performance and infringement of creditors’ rights;
Worsened access to external liquidity sources.
RATING COMPONENTS
Standalone creditworthiness assessment (SCA): a-.
Support: none.
ISSUE RATINGS
Bond issued by Borjomi Finance LLC, series 001P-01 (RU000A102SK1), maturity date: February 20, 2026, issue volume: RUB 7 bln — A-(RU).
Rationale. The issue represents senior unsecured debt of Borjomi Finance LLC, a subsidiary of Rissa Investments Limited. The credit rating is based on a guarantee from Rissa Investments Limited, public irrevocable offers from the Group’s trading companies (IDS Borjomi Beverages Co. N. V., Curacao, and LLC “IDS Borjomi (Moscow)”), its plants in Russia (LLC “Aqua Star” (Kostroma) and LLC “Edelweiss L” (Lipetsk)), and Georgia-based IDS Borjomi Georgia LLC. Despite the existence of suretyships from the key operating companies of Rissa Investments Limited, ACRA applied the detailed approach to assess the reimbursement rate due to a significant amount of secured debt and a noticeable time difference between the dates of bond purchases for different groups of offerors. According to this approach and the Agency’s methodology, the recovery rate for the issue is category II, and therefore the credit rating of the issue is equal to the credit rating of Rissa Investments Limited, i.e. A-(RU). Under the detailed approach, the Agency took into account the difficulties that IDS Borjomi Georgia LLC has come up against when making dollar-denominated settlements. In the event of a prolonged suspension of operations of the plants of the Georgian segment, ACRA may use the assets of the Russian segment alone to determine the reimbursement rate. These assets have a higher share of secured bank debt and higher leverage compared to the Group as a whole, which may lead to a downgrade of the issue rating relative to the Company’s rating.
REGULATORY DISCLOSURE
The credit ratings of Rissa Investments Limited and the bond (RU000A102SK1) issued by Borjomi Finance LLC, a subsidiary of Rissa Investments Limited, have been assigned under the national scale for the Russian Federation based on the Methodology for Credit Ratings Assignment to Non-Financial Corporations under the National Scale for the Russian Federation and the Key Concepts Used by the Analytical Credit Rating Agency within the Scope of Its Rating Activities. The Methodology for Assigning Credit Ratings to Individual Issues of Financial Instruments on the National Scale for the Russian Federation was also applied to assign the credit rating to the above issue.
The credit ratings of Rissa Investments Limited and the bond (RU000A102SK1) issued by Borjomi Finance LLC were published by ACRA for the first time on May 21, 2020 and February 26, 2021, respectively. The credit rating of Rissa Investments Limited and its outlook and the credit rating of the bond (RU000A102SK1) issued by Borjomi Finance LLC are expected to be revised within one year following the publication date of this press release.
The credit ratings were assigned based on data provided by Rissa Investments Limited, information from publicly available sources, and ACRA’s own databases. The credit ratings are solicited, and Rissa Investments Limited participated in their assignment.
Deviations from approved methodologies: the Geographical Diversification factor was assessed with a deviation from the Methodology for Credit Ratings Assignment to Non-Financial Corporations under the National Scale for the Russian Federation taking into account the geography of the Company’s operations and its key sales markets.
In assigning the credit ratings, ACRA used only information, the quality and reliability of which was, in ACRA’s opinion, appropriate and sufficient to apply the methodologies.
ACRA provided no additional services to Rissa Investments Limited and Borjomi Finance LLC. No conflicts of interest were discovered in the course of credit rating assignment.

The credit rating of RKS-Holding LLC (hereinafter, RKS-Holding, the Company, or the Group) has been upgraded due to the decrease in the Group’s debt load as a result of the withdrawal of JSC Vladimirskie Kommunalnye Sistemy with a high level of debt from its perimeter. The Company’s operations are still characterized by very low industry risks and strong market positions, while its credit rating is based on an average business profile, acceptable profitability and a strong liquidity position, coupled with low cash flow assessment. Previously, the Company was assigned A-(RU), outlook Stable.  
RKS-Holding is one of the largest utilities operators in Russia providing water supply and water sewage services (key business) as well as heating and electric power supplies. The Company provides its services in eight regions of Russia. The Group comprises companies operating on the basis of lease/concession agreements and other operating assets supporting its activities. RKS-Holding is owned by PJSC T Plus.
KEY RATING ASSESSMENT FACTORS
Lower debt burden as a result of the withdrawal of JSC Vladimirskie Kommunalnye Sistemy, which had a higher debt burden compared to the average of the Group’s enterprises, from the Group’s consolidation perimeter. As a result of these changes, according to ACRA preliminary estimates, the ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments was 1.6–1.7x by the end of 2018, while the ratio of FFO before net interest payments to interest payments rose to 5.5–5.8x. This indicates a low level of debt burden, which will be maintained in 2019-2021, according to ACRA forecasts.  
Strong liquidity position. The Group’s debt structure is fairly well diversified in terms of repayment periods. In 2019, the Company will need to repay nearly RUB 1 bln (20% of the current debt). The Company’s liquidity is supported by unused credit limits in the amount of RUB 3.7 bln as of early 2019 (total loan debt is RUB 5.2 bln). According to ACRA estimates, the short-term liquidity ratio will be rather high in 2019-2021 — around 2.4x.
Low free cash flow and low capital expenditures. According to ACRA estimates, the ratio of the Company’s capital expenditures to revenues amounted to 9% in 2018, and will be at 10-11% in the next three years. It points to a relatively low burden in terms of cash flow expenses. At the same time, the weighted average FCF margin (free cash flow) for 2016-2021 is close to zero, according to ACRA forecasts.
Infrastructure monopoly with a small-scale business and heightened regulatory risk. Services that the Group provides are qualified as a natural monopoly: there is virtually no competition in the utilities sector, service prices are relatively predictable, and demand is non-elastic and almost unresponsive to price changes. The Company’s position can be strengthened should there be an increase in the share of regions in its revenues, in which concession agreements are concluded between the local administration and the Company, which, according to ACRA estimates, could lower the risk of replacement of the water supply and sewage operator. Currently, the share of such regions is below 50% of the Company's revenues. Supply risks are related to non-payments by consumers. Receivables impairment reserve to revenues ratio is around 2% (the above share of non-payments is incorporated into the rates). The Company’s regulatory risk is driven by a high share of the regulated rates in the ultimate service price coupled with low business profitability: in 2019-2021, the average FFO margin before net interest payments and taxes will be maintained at the current level, i.e. 11–13%. Regions in which the Group operates exhibit a moderate level of socio-economic development, according to ACRA estimates. The scale of business remains small: the ratio of FFO before net interest payments and taxes will be RUB 3.2–3.4 bln in 2019–2021, according to ACRA estimates. The investment program is small in view of the business scale, but it is subject to revision if the investment component is not fully incorporated into the rates.
KEY ASSUMPTIONS
In 2018-2020, the average tariff indexation rate to equal inflation (at 4% on average);
Maintaining the receivables impairment reserve to revenues ratio within 3%;
 Implementation of capital development program by the Company in compliance with the announced deadlines and scope (around 10% of revenues);
Maintaining the current leverage ratio (total debt to FFO before net interest payments ratio below 2.0x);
Retaining current access to external liquidity sources.
POTENTIAL OUTLOOK OR RATING CHANGE FACTORS
The Stable outlook assumes that the rating will most likely stay unchanged within the 12 to 18-month horizon.
A positive rating action may be prompted by:
FFO margin before net interest payments climbing above 15%;
FCF margin rising above 5%;
Scale of business increasing substantially;
FFO before net interest payments to interest payments ratio rising above 10x and total debt declining below 1.0х FFO;
Share of regions, where concession agreements between the local administration and the Company were concluded, increasing above 70%. 
A negative rating action may be prompted by:
Higher regulatory risks;
Receivables impairment reserve to revenues ratio rising above 3%;
An increase in leverage (total debt rising above 2.0x FFO) and a decline in the FFO before net interest payments to interest payments ratio below 5.0x, coupled with the simultaneous deterioration of the debt profile;
A decline in FFO margin before net interest payments and taxes below 5%;
A decline in liquidity position.
Rating components
Standalone creditworthiness assessment (SCA): a.
Adjustments: none.
Support: none.
ISSUE RATINGS
No outstanding issues have been rated.
REGULATORY DISCLOSURE
The credit rating has been assigned under the national scale for the Russian Federation and is based on the Methodology for Credit Ratings Assignment to Non-Financial Corporations under the National Scale for the Russian Federation, and the Key Concepts Used by the Analytical Credit Rating Agency within the Scope of Its Rating Activities.
For the first time, the credit rating of RKS-Holding LLC was published by ACRA on March 27, 2018. The credit rating and its outlook are expected to be revised within one year following the publication date of this press release.
The assigned credit rating is based on data provided by RKS-Holding LLC, information from publicly available sources as well as ACRA’s own databases. The credit rating is solicited, and RKS-Holding LLC participated in its assignment.
No material discrepancies between the provided data and data officially disclosed by RKS-Holding LLC in its financial statements have been discovered.
ACRA provided no additional services to RKS-Holding LLC. No conflicts of interest were discovered in the course of credit rating assignment.

The credit rating of PJSC VimpelCom (hereinafter VimpelCom, or the Company) stems from the strong operational risk profile, which, in turn is based on the wide coverage network in the Russian Federation, strong market position, and moderate diversification of services. ACRA has changed the rating outlook from Developing to Stable because it positively assesses changes in the corporate governance system. At the same time, the Agency continues to monitor the process of formalizing the Company’s development strategy.
The Company’s financial risk profile is characterized by very high scores for size of business, profitability, and liquidity. Despite the growth of debt in 2023, the Company’s leverage is assessed as low due to the strong quality assessment of the loan portfolio. The assessment of the debt service factor, in turn, has been lowered from medium to low amid growth of interest payments.
VimpelCom is one of the largest Russian communication operators, providing services under the beeline brand. The Company has a wide coverage network in the Russian Federation. As of September 30, 2023, the mobile communications segment had 44 mln clients, while the broadband internet segment had 2.9 mln clients.
KEY ASSESSMENT FACTORS
The strong operational risk profile is determined by the moderate diversification of revenues — the Company provides mobile and fixed-line communication services and continues to develop digital services. Despite its exit from the Kazakh market, VimpelCom retains a wide coverage network in Russia and is one of the four largest communications operators in the country. The Company’s market position is also assessed as moderately high in the broadband segment. The telecommunications infrastructure operated by VimpelCom generally belongs to the latest technological generation typical for the Russian market. ACRA continues to monitor the process of formalizing the Company’s development strategy and positively assesses changes in VimpelCom’s corporate governance system, in particular, the creation of a board of directors with a high share of independent directors. This served as the reason to change the credit rating outlook from Developing to Stable. The Company maintaining a high level of financial transparency also had a positive impact on its rating.
Very high assessment of business size and profitaiblity. As of September 30, 2023, VimpelCom’s mobile communications segment had 44 mln clients, while the broadband internet segment had 2.9 mln clients. The outflow of clients in the mobile communications segment which was observed in recent years, stopped in mid-2023. The stabilization of the subscriber base coupled with confidently growing ARPU leads us to expect that revenues increased to about RUB 300 bln in 2023. At the same time, FFO before fixed payments and taxes may be around RUB 130 bln, which corresponds to the highest score for business size. As of 9M 2023, the Company’s revenues had grown by 5% compared to the same period in 2022. ACRA expects revenue growth rates to remain at 4–5% and the FFO margin before fixed payments and taxes to be at least 40% in 2023–2026.
Low leverage and deteriorating coverage of fixed payments. VimpelCom raised RUB 172 bln of financing in 2023 as part of the management’s deal to buy the Company from VEON. According to ACRA’s estimates, this led to the ratio of total debt adjusted for lease to FFO before fixed payments increasing from 2.6x at the end of 2022 to 3.4x at the end of 2023. The Company’s loan portfolio includes ruble-denominated exchange-traded and commercial bonds with fixed rates ranging from 7.3% to 11.35% per annum that are due in 2025–2030. A third of the Company’s loan portfolio — RUB 90 bln — is ruble-denominated bank financing that is due in 2030–2031 and has been provided at fixed rates (10.75–11% per annum) for the next one to three years and will then switch to floating rates. The medium indicator for total debt to FFO before fixed payments coupled with the strong quality assessment of the loan portfolio allows the Company’s leverage to be characterized as low. The Agency expects adjusted total debt to FFO before fixed payments may decline to 3.0x by 2026.
The FFO to fixed payments ratio declining below 2.5x in 2023, which ACRA expected, led to a downgrade in the debt service assessment. The Agency forecasts this indicator to be the range of 2.2–2.4x in 2024–2025 and expects it to return to a level exceeding 2.5x in 2026.
Very strong liquidity indicator and medium cash flow assessment. ACRA assesses the Company’s debt repayment schedule as comfortable — major repayments are expected from 2026 onward and will peak in 2029. In March 2023, VimpelCom signed an agreement to alter the conditions of its loan contracts with Sberbank (ACRA rating AAA(RU), outlook Stable) and JSC “ALFA-BANK” (ACRA rating AA+(RU), outlook Stable), which allowed the Company to considerably defer its loan repayments. VimpelCom has a credit line and significant balances of cash and equivalents that are available after the partial redemption of VEON’s bonds in October 2023. The Agency expects that the Company had a positive free cash flow (FCF) in 2023 against the backdrop of consistently high operating margins and the Company’s decision to defer dividend payments. ACRA assumes that this indicator will experience increased volatility due to the uneven distribution of capital expenditures as a result of the complicated logistics of equipment delivery, but in general it expects the FCF margin to be in the range of 3–6% in the medium term.
KEY ASSUMPTIONS
Average annual growth of revenues of at 4–5% in 2023–2026;
FFO margin before fixed payments and taxes at no lower than 40%;
Stable subscriber base and average annual growth of ARPU at 4–5%;
Possibility to acquire telecommunications equipment;
Continued access to external liquidity sources;
Completion of the formalization of the Company’s strategy in the event of no significant changes to it;
No new borrowings until 2026.
POTENTIAL OUTLOOK OR RATING CHANGE FACTORS
The Stable outlook assumes that the rating will highly likely stay unchanged within the 12 to 18-month horizon.
A positive rating action may be prompted by:
Weighted average ratio of total debt adjusted for lease to FFO before fixed payments falling below 2.0x and weighted average ratio of FFO before fixed payments to fixed payments consistently above 2.5x;
FCF margin exceeding 10%.
A negative rating action may be prompted by:
Ratio of total debt adjusted for lease to FFO before fixed payments exceeding 3.5x;
Weighted average ratio of FFO before fixed payments to fixed payments declining below 1.0x;
FFO margin before fixed payments and taxes declining below 40%;
Consistently negative FCF;
Deterioration of corporate governance practices and financial transparency;
Considerably narrower access to external liquidity sources.
RATING COMPONENTS
Standalone creditworthiness assessment (SCA): aa-.
Support: none.
ISSUE RATINGS
No outstanding issues have been rated.
REGULATORY DISCLOSURE
The credit rating has been assigned to PJSC VimpelCom under the national scale for the Russian Federation based on the Methodology for Credit Ratings Assignment to Non-Financial Corporations under the National Scale for the Russian Federation and the Key Concepts Used by the Analytical Credit Rating Agency within the Scope of Its Rating Activities.
The credit rating of PJSC VimpelCom was published by ACRA for the first time on June 16, 2022. The credit rating and its outlook are expected to be revised within one year following the publication date of this press release.
The credit rating was assigned based on data provided by PJSC VimpelCom, information from publicly available sources, and ACRA’s own databases. The credit rating is solicited and PJSC VimpelCom participated in its assignment.
In assigning the credit rating, ACRA used only information, the quality and reliability of which were, in ACRA’s opinion, appropriate and sufficient to apply the methodologies.
ACRA provided additional services to PJSC VimpelCom. No conflicts of interest were discovered in the course of credit rating assignment.

The credit rating of TGC-14 PJSC (hereinafter, TGC-14 or the Company) has been downgraded due to the following combination of factors. Higher growth of leverage than previously set out by the Company in its projections. Worsening of the corporate governance assessment on the back of the arrest of owners and members of the board of directors of the Company and the decisions of the courts of the Republic of Buryatia and the Zabaykalsky Krai to invalidate the orders of the tariff services on setting heat tariffs for TGC-14’s consumers. In ACRA’s opinion, these events have resulted in risks for the Company of both a reduction in tariffs already established for it and the return by the Company of part of the funds previously received through tariffs. According to the Company: (1) it plans to appeal the said decision of the court of the Republic of Buryatia in cassation; (2) currently, there is no corresponding replacement act of the tariff service of the Republic of Buryatia; (3) the Company has sent information about TGC-14’s expenditures that were not taken into account in the tariffs to the tariff service of the Republic of Buryatia; (4) the Company filed a claim with the Zabaykalsky Krai Court against the public legal entity Zabaykalsky Krai (represented by the Ministry of Finance of the Zabaykalsky Krai and the Ministry of Housing and Public Utilities, Energy, Digitalization and Communications of the Zabaykalsky Krai) for the recovery of lost revenues arising as a result of state regulation of tariffs for thermal energy from 2022–2023 (case No. A78-9486/2025). In addition, taking into account the situation described above involving the owners and senior management of TGC-14, the liquidity score has been lowered because in ACRA’s opinion, the Company may encounter difficulties in obtaining new debt. At the same time, the Company has begun implementing projects within the framework of the CSC NGF (competitive selection of capacity of new generating facilities) for the construction of two power units at its Ulan-Ude CHP-2, which are characterized by high capital intensity and penalties for failure to comply with the parameters of their implementation. According to the Company, it is currently carrying out large-scale work on the implementation of measures aimed at applying instruments for optimizing expenses on financing the specified projects. Uncertainty related to the estimated costs of projects (the growth of which may potentially result in the need for additional borrowings), as well as to the timing, formats and results of the implementation of the above measures (including the parameters of project financing), is the reason why the outlook on the Company’s credit rating is Negative.
At the same time, TGC-14 maintains a strong position in heat supply services in the area of operation (the Republic of Buryatia and the Zabaykalsky Krai), as well as an adequate business assessment. The geography assessment reflects the level of socioeconomic development of TGC-14’s regions of presence. The profitability assessment is above medium. The Company’s credit rating is constrained by, among other things, the size of business, systemic regulatory risks, and the metrics for leverage, debt service, free cash flow (FCF), and capital expenditures related to a significant increase in the Company’s capital investment program.
TGC-14 is a regional producer and supplier of electricity and heat in the Republic of Buryatia and the Zabaykalsky Krai. The installed electric power of its production assets is 650 MW, the heating power is 2,866 Gcal/h, and the length of heating networks is 941 km. The Company’s main shareholder is FEMC JSC. In December 2023, FEMC JSC was vested with the powers of the sole executive body of TGC-14.
KEY ASSESSMENT FACTORS
Strong position in heat supply. TGC-14 is the largest heat supplier in the Zabaykalsky Krai and the Republic of Buryatia, with market shares of 26% and 31%, respectively. The Company is the sole heat supplier in Chita and Ulan-Ude. The Company owns seven power plants, 45 boiler houses, and heat supply networks. TGC-14 also produces about 35% and 11% of the electricity in the Zabaykalsky Krai and the Republic of Buryatia, respectively.
Adequate business assessment. The Company’s regulatory risk is assessed as moderate, since most of its revenues are subject to tariff regulation, which may be affected by non-economic factors or untimely tariff changes. The current regulatory framework provides for coverage of the operational and capital expenses of TGC-14, as well as a weighted FFO margin at around 15–20% before net interest payments and taxes, according to the Agency’s calculations. The sales risks are assessed as low. The quality of the Company’s fixed assets is assessed as medium.
Geography and corporate governance. The geographic diversification assessment is determined by the degree of economic development of the regions of presence. TGC-14’s development strategy until 2025 inclusive is aimed at maintaining and upgrading the operating assets and improving production and economic efficiency. The Company is currently working on a new, post-2025 strategy, aimed at broadening production capacity in the regions of presence, digitalization, and developing human capital. The Company has an internal control and risk management service. Each risk is assigned a risk owner who is responsible for identifying it. After the accident at the Company’s Ulan-Ude CHP-1, the approach to risk identification and specification was changed in order to ensure effective risk management and prevention. In December 2021, the Company’s shareholder structure changed — ownership of TGC-14 passed from the subsidiaries of JSCo “RZD” to FEMC JSC. The shareholders of FEMC JSC were heavily involved in the Company’s activities. In 2024, FEMC JSC sold part of its stock, after which its stake declined from 92.44% to 79.32%. TGC-14 has a management board, as well as a board of directors consisting of 11 members, five of whom are independent. In 2024, the Company changed its financial statement auditor, which led to the need to recalculate the Company’s IFRS financial statement data for 2023. TGC-14 discloses IFRS financial statements and annual reports in compliance with industry standards. The corporate governance assessment has been downgraded due to the arrest of owners and members of the board of directors of the Company and the decisions of the courts of the Republic of Buryatia and the Zabaykalsky Krai to invalidate the orders of the tariff services on setting heat tariffs for TGC-14’s consumers. These events, in ACRA’s opinion, have created risks of both a reduction in tariffs already established for the Company and the return by the Company of part of the funds previously received through tariffs, which will have a direct impact on the Company’s incoming cash flow.
Business size and profitability. The Company’s FFO before net interest payments and taxes, as calculated according to ACRA’s methodology, grew to RUB 3.8 bln in 2024 from RUB 2.7 bln in 2023, mainly due to the receipt of subsidies. The Agency expects the weighted average value of the indicator to be RUB 5.9 bln in 2025–2027, mainly due to subsidies to compensate for tariff differences. The Company’s FFO margin before net interest payments and taxes grew to 20% in 2024 (vs. 15% in 2023), and ACRA expects the weighted average margin to be 21% in 2025–2027 if subsidies are received as per TGC-14’s calculations. The Agency has revised its projections of the Company’s financial metrics based on its financial statements for 6M and 9M 2025.
Leverage and coverage. As of September 30, 2025, the Company’s debt portfolio amounted to RUB 20.3 bln and was diversified by lenders. Currently, TGC-14 has no guarantees or sureties issued. At the end of 2024, total debt (including pension obligations and leases) amounted to RUB 9.9 bln or 2.8x of FFO before net interest payments. According to ACRA’s estimates, leverage is expected to grow to an average weighted 5.8x in 2025–2027.
At the end of 2024, the coverage ratio (FFO before net interest payments to interest payments) amounted to 3.1x. ACRA expects this ratio to decrease to an average of 0.9x in 2025–2027, including due to an increase in TGC-14’s leverage, which drives debt servicing costs up.
Liquidity and FCF. When assessing liquidity, the Agency did not take into account the possible free limits of TGC-14 because in ACRA’s opinion, the Company may face difficulties in obtaining new debt due to the arrest of its owners and management. In 2024, the Company’s FCF calculated as per ACRA’s methodology amounted to RUB -0.7 bln vs. RUB 2.4 bln a year earlier. This decline was mainly due to higher dividend payments and capital expenditures. The Agency expects FCF to be negative in 2025–2027 due to growth of capital expenditures.
The ratio of the Company’s investments to revenues was 16% in 2024 (vs. 10% in 2023). ACRA expects the weighted average volume of investments to grow to 46% in 2025–2027.
KEY ASSUMPTIONS
Implementation of the Company’s capital investment program in 2025–2027 as planned;
Dividends to be paid in 2025 as declared, and FEMC JSC to repay all of its debt obligations to the Company out of these dividends by the end of this year;
No dividend payments from 2026 onwards;
The Company maintaining its current liquidity positon;
The Company receiving annual subsidies of at least RUB 1,455 mln to cover inter-tariff differences in 2025–2027;
Permanent balance of at least RUB 3.5 bln in the Company’s accounts needed to redeem the bond issue in 2026;
The Company obtaining a positive state expert opinion on the projects for the construction of two power units at Ulan-Ude CHP-2 (for unit No. 1 — July 2025, for unit No. 2 — March 2026);
State expert commission confirming the estimated cost of no more than RUB 38.2 bln for the projects for the construction of two power units at Ulan-Ude CHP-2 (for unit No. 1 — July 2025, for unit No. 2 — March 2026);
The Company attracting at least RUB 30.4 bln of long-term financing for the implementation of the projects for the construction of two power units at Ulan-Ude CHP-2 until January 1, 2026.
POTENTIAL OUTLOOK OR RATING CHANGE FACTORS
The Negative outlook assumes that the rating will highly likely be downgraded within the 12 to 18-month horizon.
A positive rating action may be prompted by:
Weighted leverage declining below 4.0x;
Weighted coverage exceeding 2.5x.
A negative rating action may be prompted by:
Weighted leverage exceeding 6.0x and weighted coverage declining below 1.0x;
The Company losing its status of the sole heat supplier in Chita and Ulan-Ude;
Dividends exceeding the declared amounts;
The Company not receiving annual subsidies of at least RUB 1,455 mln to cover inter-tariff differences in 2025–2027;
No permanent balance of at least RUB 3.5 bln in the Company’s accounts needed to redeem the bond issue in 2026;
The Company not obtaining a positive state expert opinion on the projects for the construction of two power units at Ulan-Ude CHP-2 (for unit No. 1 — July 2025, for unit No. 2 — March 2026);
State expert commission not confirming the estimated cost of no more than RUB 38.2 bln for the projects for the construction of two power units at Ulan-Ude CHP-2 (for unit No. 1 — July 2025, for unit No. 2 — March 2026);
The Company not raising at least RUB 30.4 bln for the implementation of the projects for the construction of two power units at Ulan-Ude CHP-2 by January 1, 2026.
RATING COMPONENTS
Standalone creditworthiness assessment (SCA): bb.
Support: none.
ISSUE RATINGS
No outstanding issues have been rated.
REGULATORY DISCLOSURE
The credit rating has been assigned to TGC-14 PJSC based on the following methodologies: the Methodology for Assigning Credit Ratings to Non-Financial Corporations under the National Scale for the Russian Federation to calculate the SCA and determine the credit rating and the credit rating outlook of TGC-14 PJSC under the national scale for the Russian Federation and the Key Concepts Used by the Analytical Credit Rating Agency within the Scope of Its Rating Activities to ensure consistent and uniform application of ACRA’s methodologies, rating scales, models, and key rating assumptions. The principles of the following methodologies were also applied: the Methodology for Assigning Credit Ratings to Regions and Municipal Entities of the Russian Federation to assess the economic development of the region of presence of the rated entity.
The credit rating of TGC-14 PJSC assigned under the national scale for the Russian Federation was published by ACRA for the first time on May 17, 2023.
The credit rating and its outlook are expected to be revised within one year.
The credit rating was assigned based on data provided by TGC-14 PJSC, information from publicly available sources, and ACRA’s own databases. The rating analysis was performed using the IFRS accounting (financial) statements of TGC-14 PJSC as of December 31, 2024.
The credit rating is solicited and TGC-14 PJSC participated in its assignment.
In assigning the credit rating, ACRA used only information, the quality and reliability of which were, in ACRA’s opinion, appropriate and sufficient to apply the methodologies.
ACRA provided the following additional services to TGC-14 PJSC during the year preceding the rating action: assessment of the obligations of companies/regions, municipalities and countries for compliance with sustainable development principles from May 13, 2022 to the present.
No conflicts of interest were discovered in the course of credit rating assignment.

The credit rating of TGC-14 PJSC (hereinafter, TGC-14 or the Company) has been downgraded due to the projected growth of leverage and debt service costs, growing number of related-party transactions (Far Eastern Management Company JSC (FEMC JSC) and Spetsavtokhoziaystvo JSC), and increasing expenditures on capital investments as the Company enters the investment cycle of development. The Company has begun, among other things, implementing projects within the framework of the CSC NGF (competitive selection of capacity of new generating facilities) for the construction of two power units at its Ulan-Ude CHP-2, which are characterized by high capital intensity and penalties for failure to comply with the parameters of their implementation.
TGC-14 is currently carrying out large-scale work on the implementation of measures aimed at applying instruments for optimizing expenses on financing the said projects. Uncertainty related to the estimated costs of projects, which increase may potentially result in the need for additional borrowings, as well as to the timing, formats and results of the implementation of the above measures, including the parameters of project financing, is the reason why the outlook has been changed to Negative.
At the same time, TGC-14 maintains a strong position in heat supply services in the area of operation (the Republic of Buryatia and the Zabaykalsky Krai), as well as its adequate business profile and corporate governance that meets the industry standards. Geographic diversification reflects the level of socioeconomic development of TGC-14’s regions of presence. The profitability assessment is above medium. The Company’s credit rating is constrained by, among other things, the size of business, systemic regulatory risks, and the projected metrics for leverage, debt service, free cash flow (FCF), and capital expenditures related to a significant increase in the Company’s capital expenditure program.
TGC-14 is a regional producer and supplier of electricity and heat in the Republic of Buryatia and the Zabaykalsky Krai. The installed electric power of its production assets is 650 MW, the heating power is 2,866 Gcal/h, and the length of heating networks is 941 km. The Company’s main shareholder is FEMC JSC. In December 2023, FEMC JSC was vested with the powers of the sole executive body of TGC-14.
KEY ASSESSMENT FACTORS
Strong position in heat supply. TGC-14 is the largest heat supplier in the Zabaykalsky Krai and the Republic of Buryatia, with a market share of 26% and 31%, respectively. The Company is the sole heat supplier in Chita and Ulan-Ude. The Company owns seven power plants, 45 boiler houses, and heat supply networks. TGC-14 also produces about 35% and 11% of the electricity in the Zabaykalsky Krai and the Republic of Buryatia, respectively.
Adequate business profile. The Company’s regulatory risk is assessed as moderate, since most of its revenues are subject to tariff regulation, which may be affected by non-economic factors or untimely tariff changes. The current regulatory framework provides for coverage of the operational and capital expenses of TGC-14, as well as a weighted FFO margin at around 15–20% before net interest payments and taxes, according to the Agency’s calculations. The sales risks are assessed as low. The quality of the Company’s fixed assets is assessed as medium.
Adequate geographic diversification and corporate governance. The geographic diversification assessment is determined by the degree of economic development of the regions of presence. TGC-14’s development strategy until 2025 inclusive is aimed at maintaining and upgrading the operating assets and improving production and economic efficiency. The Company is currently working on a new, post-2025 strategy, aimed at broadening production capacity in the regions of presence, digitalization, and developing human capital. The Company has an internal control and risk management service. Each risk is assigned a risk owner who is responsible for identifying it. After the accident at the Company’s Ulan-Ude CHP-1, the approach to risk identification and specification was changed in order to ensure effective risk management and prevention. In December 2021, the Company’s shareholder structure changed — ownership of TGC-14 passed from the subsidiaries of JSCo “RZD” to FEMC JSC. The shareholders of FEMC JSC are deeply involved in the Company’s activities. In 2024, FEMC JSC sold part of its stock, after which its stake declined from 92.44% to 79.28%. TGC-14 has a management board, as well as a board of directors consisting of 11 members, five of whom are independent. In 2024, the Company changed its financial statement auditor, which led to the need to recalculate the Company’s IFRS financial statement data for 2023. TGC-14 discloses IFRS financial statements and annual reports in compliance with industry standards.
Business size and profitability. The Company’s FFO before net interest payments and taxes, as calculated according to ACRA’s methodology, grew to RUB 3.8 bln in 2024 from RUB 2.7 bln in 2023. The growth was mainly driven by the receipt of subsidies. The Agency expects the weighted average value of the indicator to be RUB 5.7 bln in 2025–2027, mainly due to subsidies to compensate for tariff differences. The Company’s FFO margin before net interest payments and taxes grew to 20% in 2024 (vs. 15% in 2023), and ACRA expects the weighted average margin to be 25% in 2025–2027 if subsidies are received as per the Company’s calculations.
Leverage and coverage. As of March 5, 2025, the Company’s debt portfolio was estimated at RUB 13.8 bln and was diversified by lenders. Currently, TGC-14 has no guarantees or sureties issued. At the end of 2024, the total debt of TGC-14 (including pension obligations and leases) amounted to RUB 9.9 bln or 2.8x of FFO before net interest payments. In 2025–2027, according to ACRA’s estimates, the Company’s leverage is expected to grow to an average weighted 4.4x.
At the end of 2024, the coverage ratio (FFO before net interest payments to interest payments) amounted to 3.1x. ACRA expects this ratio to decrease to an average of 1.2x in 2025–2027 due to an increase in TGC-14’s leverage and key rate hikes by the Bank of Russia, which drive debt service costs up.
Liquidity and FCF. As of March 5, 2025, the Company had free debt limits and cash. In 2024, the Company’s FCF calculated according to ACRA’s methodology amounted to RUB -0.7 bln vs. RUB 2.4 bln a year earlier. This decline was mainly due to higher dividend payments and capital expenditures. The Agency expects that FCF to be negative in 2025–2027 due to growth of capital expenditures.
The ratio of the Company’s investments to revenues was 16% in 2024 (vs. 10% in 2023). ACRA expects that the weighted average volume of investments will grow to 48% in 2025–2027.
KEY ASSUMPTIONS
Implementation of the Company’s capital investment program in 2025–2027 as planned;
Dividends to be paid in 2025 as declared, and FEMC JSC to repay all of its debt obligations to the Company out of these dividends this year;
No dividend payments from 2026 onwards;
The Company maintaining its current liquidity positon;
The Company receiving annual subsidies of at least RUB 1,455 mln to cover inter-tariff differences in 2025–2027;
Permanent balance of RUB 3.5 bln in the Company’s accounts needed to redeem the bond issue in 2026;
The Company obtaining a positive state expert opinion on the projects for the construction of two power units at Ulan-Ude CHP-2 (for unit No. 1 — July 2025, for unit No. 2 — March 2026);
State expert commission confirming the estimated cost of no more than RUB 38.2 bln for the projects for the construction of two power units at Ulan-Ude CHP-2 (for unit No. 1 — July 2025, for unit No. 2 — March 2026);
The Company attracting at least RUB 30.4 bln for the implementation of the projects for the construction of two power units at Ulan-Ude CHP-2 until January 1, 2026.
POTENTIAL OUTLOOK OR RATING CHANGE FACTORS
The Negative outlook assumes that the rating will highly likely be downgraded within the 12 to 18-month horizon.
A positive rating action may be prompted by:
Weighted leverage (ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments) declining below 2.0x;
Weighted coverage (FFO before net interest payments to interest payments) exceeding 2.5x.
A negative rating action may be prompted by:
Weighted ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments exceeding 6.0x;
Weighted coverage declining below 1.0x;
The Company losing its status of the sole heat supplier in Chita and Ulan-Ude;
Dividends in 2025 exceeding the declared amounts;
FEMC JSC failing to repay all of its debt obligations to the Company in 2025;
Significant deterioration of access to external liquidity sources;
The Company not receiving annual subsidies of at least RUB 1,455 mln to cover inter-tariff differences in 2025–2027;
No permanent balance of RUB 3.5 bln in the Company’s accounts needed to redeem the bond issue in 2026;
The Company not obtaining a positive state expert opinion on the projects for the construction of two power units at Ulan-Ude CHP-2 (for unit No. 1 — July 2025, for unit No. 2 — March 2026);
State expert commission not confirming the estimated cost of no more than RUB 38.2 bln for the projects for the construction of two power units at Ulan-Ude CHP-2 (for unit No. 1 — July 2025, for unit No. 2 — March 2026);
The Company not attracting at least RUB 30.4 bln for the implementation of the projects for the construction of two power units at Ulan-Ude CHP-2 until January 1, 2026.
RATING COMPONENTS
Standalone creditworthiness assessment (SCA): bbb.
Support: none.
ISSUE RATINGS
No outstanding issues have been rated.
REGULATORY DISCLOSURE
The credit rating has been assigned to TGC-14 PJSC under the national scale for the Russian Federation based on the Methodology for Assigning Credit Ratings to Non-Financial Corporations under the National Scale for the Russian Federation and the Key Concepts Used by the Analytical Credit Rating Agency within the Scope of Its Rating Activities.
The assessment of the economic development of the rated entity’s regions of presence was determined based on the principles of the Methodology for Assigning Credit Ratings to Regions and Municipal Entities of the Russian Federation.
The credit rating of TGC-14 PJSC was published by ACRA for the first time on May 17, 2023. The credit rating of TGC-14 PJSC and its outlook are expected to be revised within one year following the publication date of this press release.
The credit rating was assigned based on data provided by TGC-14 PJSC, information from publicly available sources, and ACRA’s own databases. The credit rating is solicited and TGC-14 PJSC participated in its assignment.
In assigning the credit rating, ACRA used only information, the quality and reliability of which were, in ACRA’s opinion, appropriate and sufficient to apply the methodologies.
The decision to downgrade the credit rating of TGC-14 PJSC to BBB(RU) and to change the outlook to Negative was made at a repeated meeting of the rating committee following ACRA’s review of a reasoned appeal by the rated entity against the decision of the initial rating committee to downgrade the credit rating of TGC-14 PJSC to BBB-(RU) and to change the outlook to Developing in view of the improved liquidity assessment. TGC-14 PJSC has provided new additional information on potential sources of liquidity and compensation for inter-tariff differences. As a result, the repeated meeting of the rating committee has taken into account the abovementioned new additional information when determining the credit rating. Other rating factors have not changed.
ACRA provided additional services to TGC-14 PJSC. No conflicts of interest were discovered in the course of credit rating assignment.

The credit rating of PJSC Evropeyskaya Elektrotekhnica (hereinafter, Evropeyskaya Elektrotekhnica, the Company, or the Group) has been upgraded to BBB(RU), outlook Stable, because FFO before net interest payments and taxes has exceeded RUB 500 mln, and the quality of the current contract base has improved in terms of larger volumes and longer contract performance periods, which supports the conditions for receiving funds in the forecast period.
The Company’s credit rating is based on its good liquidity, high debt coverage, and low leverage. At the same time, ACRA notes the lower than average size of the Company’s business compared to the largest Russian companies, its medium cash flow, and assesses its market position as modest in view of the high granularity of the Russian engineering market.
Evropeyskaya Elektrotekhnica is one of Russia’s leading companies in terms of production of energy and power supply systems, and lighting and low-current systems. Since its creation in 2004, Evropeyskaya Elektrotekhnica has transformed from a trading company into a full-fledged engineering firm with its own production facilities (41% of products and equipment sold by the Group are manufactured using its own production facilities). In 2018, the Company purchased oil and gas production facilities in the Republic of Bashkortostan, and finalized its legal title over them in 2020. The Group’s future development plans are related to the launch of a new high-margin business, sale and lease of innovative oil and gas equipment. The Company’s key shareholders are S. N. Dubenok and I. A. Kalenkov. Around 7% of the Company’s shares are in free float.
KEY ASSESSMENT FACTORS
Moderate operating risk profile assessment. The market share of Evropeyskaya Elektrotekhnica does not exceed 0.5%. However, the Company occupies a leading position in some sectors (up to 37% of the market for hot-dip galvanized reinforced cable systems and cable holders for ultra-deep mines, and up to 8% of the market for flare units). The Group’s order book covers a five-year horizon (including highly likely contracts); occasionally, some contracts may be postponed for a few months, which may result in a corresponding postponement of some revenues in a given period. However, the potential effect from postponing contracts will not exceed 10% of revenues, in ACRA’s opinion, owing to good diversification of the order book by customer. The Company’s sales markets exhibit moderate cyclicality. In 2019, the share of engineering systems in the Company’s revenues was 77%; the remaining portion was attributable to oil and gas equipment. In 2020, these shares amounted to 74% and 26%, respectively. The Company plans to increase the share of oil and gas equipment to 35% over the next few years. Dependence on subcontracting and components is generally assessed as moderate: dependence on subcontracting is minimal (about 2.5%), and the resale of components and equipment accounts for up to 60–80% of revenues. In 2021, the Company’s new site at Special Economic Zone Technopolis Moskva for producing smart electrical distribution boards and automation boards reached full production capacity. Geographical diversification of sales markets is moderate, with around 9% of products being exported, mainly oil and gas equipment. The Company opened a representative office in Cairo in 2021.
Adequate level of corporate governance. The Group’s strategy is sufficiently formalized; it is approved by the board of directors. However, it is subject to significant changes from time to time in response to developments in external factors and the market situation. The strategy includes reaching key financial and operating targets. The strategy implies a substantial increase in the share of the higher-margin business line (equipment manufacturing for oil and gas and hydrocarbon processing industries) in the Group’s total revenues. In 2020, the Company successfully underwent INTERGASCERT certification, which allows it to supply equipment to Gazprom group companies. ACRA notes that the Company has been strictly adhering to its strategy, including growth in FFO before net interest payments and taxes in 2020 (compared to 2019). The risk management system of Evropeyskaya Elektrotekhnica is assessed as moderate because currency and interest rate exposures are minimal and the purchase of components in overseas markets is offset by export revenues (natural hedge). ACRA assesses Evropeyskaya Elektrotekhnica’s corporate governance quality as adequate in view of, among other things, the involvement of the Company’s owners in its business. The Group includes seven legal entities, which is justified by the nature of its business. There are related-party transactions that are controlled by the Company’s board of directors. ACRA assesses Evropeyskaya Elektrotekhnica’s financial transparency as high: financial reporting is published on a quarterly basis, and regular meetings with investors are held, at which information on the Group’s current operations and development plans is disclosed.
Lower than average business size. ACRA estimates FFO before net interest payments and taxes at RUB 536.4 mln in 2020 (RUB 483.9 mln in 2019). The Agency expects this indicator to amount to RUB 592.5 mln in 2021 and then continue to undergo organic growth. The FFO margin before net interest payments and taxes was maintained at 15% in 2020 and, in the next few years, it will be within the range of 13–15%. This margin will be supported by growth in profitability buoyed by the increase in the share of oil and gas equipment in the Company’s sales structure. In 2021, the Company installed a unique mobile modular preliminary water discharge unit at LUKOIL’s Nazymskoye field.
Low leverage and high coverage. As of December 31, 2021, Evropeyskaya Elektrotekhnica’s debt portfolio amounted to RUB 80 mln, and all repayments were scheduled for 2022. The Company’s debt obligations consist of ruble-denominated bank loans. The debt portfolio is diversified by lenders. At the same time, the Company has enough liquidity sources to fully refinance its loan portfolio. There are no guarantees issued outside the Group’s consolidation perimeter. As of the end of 2020, the Company’s total debt was RUB 194 mln, or 0.4x as a ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments, having declined since 2019. ACRA expects total debt to remain at around this level in 2022–2023. In addition, coverage is and will be very high thanks to low total debt.
The Company’s liquidity is assessed as good. As of December 31, 2021, the Company had available credit lines totaling RUB 1.4 bln and held RUB 815 mln in its accounts.
KEY ASSUMPTIONS
Inflation averaging 4% in 2022–2023;
Annual revenue growth rates in segments in 2022–2023 at levels no lower than the average for the past three years;
2022–2023 dividends not exceeding dividend payments in 2021;
Dividend payments and investments in 2022–2023 in line with the announced volumes and deadlines.
POTENTIAL OUTLOOK OR RATING CHANGE FACTORS
The Stable outlook assumes that the rating will most likely stay unchanged within the 12 to 18-month horizon.
A positive rating action may be prompted by:
Annual weighted average FFO before net interest payments and taxes exceeding RUB 5 bln while maintaining the volume and terms of the contract base at a level no lower than the current one;
Annual weighted average FFO margin before net interest payments and taxes exceeding 25% while maintaining the volume and periods of the contract base at a level no lower than the current one;
Weighted average FCF margin exceeding 5% while maintaining the volume and terms of the contract base at a level no lower than the current one.
A negative rating action may be prompted by:
FFO before net interest payments and taxes falling below RUB 0.5 bln;
FFO margin before net interest payments and taxes falling below 8%;
Leverage (ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments) exceeding 1.0х;
Coverage (FFO before net interest payments to interest payments) falling below 10.0x;
FCF margin falling below zero;
Share of capital expenditure in revenues growing above 10%;
Significant deterioration of access to external liquidity sources;
Significant decline in volumes and periods of the contract base;
Significant growth of dividend payments as investments grow.
RATING COMPONENTS
Standalone creditworthiness assessment (SCA): bbb.
Adjustments: none.
ISSUE RATINGS
There are no outstanding issues.
REGULATORY DISCLOSURE
The credit rating has been assigned to PJSC Evropeyskaya Elektrotekhnica under the national scale for the Russian Federation based on the Methodology for Credit Ratings Assignment to Non-Financial Corporations under the National Scale for the Russian Federation and the Key Concepts Used by the Analytical Credit Rating Agency within the Scope of Its Rating Activities.
The credit rating of PJSC Evropeyskaya Elektrotekhnica was published by ACRA for the first time on October 11, 2018. The credit rating and its outlook are expected to be revised within one year following the publication date of this press release.
The credit rating was assigned based on data provided by PJSC Evropeyskaya Elektrotekhnica, information from publicly available sources, and ACRA’s own databases. The credit rating is solicited, and PJSC Evropeyskaya Elektrotekhnica participated in its assignment.
In assigning the credit rating, ACRA used only information, the quality and reliability of which was, in ACRA’s opinion, appropriate and sufficient to apply the methodologies.
ACRA provided no additional services to PJSC Evropeyskaya Elektrotekhnica. No conflicts of interest were discovered in the course of credit rating assignment.

The credit rating of Istrinskaya Syrovarnya LLC (hereinafter, Istrinskaya Syrovarnya or the Company) is based on a weak assessment of the operational risk profile. The Company is part of the informal Russian Parmesan group (hereinafter, the Group), within which production and trading assets belong to different owners. The assessment of the operational risk profile was also influenced by steady demand for the Company’s products coupled with concentration of its activities on a narrow segment, and weaker market position and brand awareness compared to major federal food retail chains, as well as a medium assessment of geographic presence.
The assessment of financial risk profile factors are determined by very high leverage, low interest and lease payment coverage amid a very low assessment of business size and moderately high profitability. ACRA assesses the Company’s liquidity and free cash flow (FCF) as medium.
Russian Parmesan is a Russian producer of cheeses located in the Moscow Region’s Istra district. It produces products under the Oleg Sirota Istrinskaya Syrovarnya brand. Istrinskaya Syrovarnya sells the Group’s products.
KEY ASSESSMENT FACTORS
Weak operational risk profile assessment. The business model of Istrinskaya Syrovarnya remains unchanged — the Company is a highly specialized retail chain that sells the products of mainly a single producer. At the same time, work in the food retail segment has a positive impact on the business profile assessment. Taking into account the fact that ACRA has analyzed the Group’s retail unit, the assessment of Istrinskaya Syrovarnya’s market position and brand awareness are still performed by way of comparison to other retail chains, including those that operate on a federal level. Due to this, the assessments of these factors, in the Agency’s opinion, continue to be low. The Agency continues to assess the Company’s geography of business as medium because it operates a regional chain, but it is concentrated on Moscow (ACRA rating AAA(RU), outlook Stable) and the Moscow Region (ACRA rating AAA (RU), outlook Stable).
ACRA continues to assess Istrinskaya Syrovarnya’s corporate governance as appropriate for the scale of its activities. The Company does not have a board of directors and corporate procedures are poorly regulated. According to the Agency’s assessments, the Company’s financial transparency is low — the Company only puts together RAS reporting (the auditor is DONAUDIT JSC). Heightened, in the Agency’s opinion, risks of the structure of the Group continue to have a significant negative impact on the assessment of the factor.
Very low assessment of business size and moderately high assessment of profitability. Istrinskaya Syrovarnya has revised its plans with regard to the number of retail points it plans to open in 2024–2026. According to the Company’s current plans, its chain will have around 110 stores in the indicated period (vs. 133 and 123 as of the end of 2022 and 2023). Previously, the Company planned to increase the number of stores to 300 by 2025. According to the Agency’s forecast, the revenues of Istrinskaya Syrovarnya may range from RUB 1.6–1.7 bln in 2024–2026, while FFO before fixed payments and taxes may be in the range of RUB 160­–180 mln. Both indicators are at a very low level as per ACRA’s methodology.
The FFO margin before fixed payments and taxes continues to be in the range of 9–10%, which, according to ACRA’s approaches corresponds to a medium assessment. In the Company’s opinion, reducing the size of the chain in favor of the most successful retail points could have a positive impact on profitability. The assessment of this factor is supported by EBITDAR per square meter, which, according to ACRA’s calculations, amounted to around RUB 100,000 in 2023, and this corresponds to a very high score and is mainly determined by the retail format. ACRA’s forecast assumes that the Company’s profitability indicators will remain at this level in 2024–2026.
Very high leverage and low coverage of interest and lease payments. As of September 20, 2024, Istrinskaya Syrovarnya’s debt portfolio included two bond issues to a total of RUB 250 mln and loans from JSC Russian Agricultural Bank (ACRA rating AA(RU), outlook Stable) of RUB 104 mln. In 2024, the Agency expects that the ratio of Istrinskaya Syrovarnya’s total debt to FFO before net interest payments to be 4.1x, and the ratio of lease-adjusted total debt to FFO before fixed payments to be 6.6x, which together correspond to very high leverage. To determine the final score for leverage, ACRA included in its calculation sureties provided by Istrinskaya Syrovarnya to related parties, the limit for which is RUB 1.2 bln, and this put additional pressure on leverage. As of July 1, 2024, sureties had been provided for RUB 806 mln of liabilities.
Debt service indicators remain low. ACRA projects that the ratio of FFO before net interest payments to interest payments may amount to 1.2x in 2024, while FFO before fixed payments to fixed (lease and interest) payments may be 1.1x, which indicates low coverage of interest and lease payments. The assessment of the Debt Service factor continues to include the premise that other entities in the Group will not use the Company’s cash flows to service their own debt. However, the credit rating of Istrinskaya Syrovarnya may be revised if this practice begins.
Medium assessments of liquidity and FCF. Istrinskaya Syrovarnya’s decision to not seriously expand its chain could lead to lower capital expenditures and investments in working capital, which in turn may enable the Company to maintain its FCF at around zero or just below zero on the condition that there are no dividend payments. On the back of no major repayments until 2026, this scenario will enable Istrinskaya Syrovarnya to have acceptable refinancing risks. At the same time, ACRA assesses the Company’s liquidity as medium due to the presence of a peak repayment period in the medium term. Istrinskaya Syrovarnya’s balance sheet reflects the debt of a related party to the Company to be repaid in 2027, which the Company views as a source of liquidity. ACRA did not take it into account when analyzing liquidity due to a lack of sufficient information on the recoverability of this loan.
KEY ASSUMPTIONS
Maintaining the current structure of the Group;
100–120 retail points in 2024–2026 while maintaining the current level of profitability;
Maintaining the current product range and supply structure;
No annual dividend payments;
The Group servicing its own debt obligations without using the Company’s cash flows;
Access to external liquidity sources.
POTENTIAL OUTLOOK OR RATING CHANGE FACTORS
The Stable outlook assumes that the rating will highly likely stay unchanged within the 12 to 18-month horizon.
A positive rating action may be prompted by:
Weighted average ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments falling below 6.0x and adjusted total debt to FFO before fixed payments falling below 5.0x;
Weighted average ratio of FFO before fixed payments to fixed payments exceeding 1.5x or weighted average ratio of FFO before net interest payments to interest payments exceeding 2.5x;
Weighted average FFO before fixed payments and taxes exceeding RUB 500 mln or revenues exceeding RUB 5 bln;
Weighted average FFO margin before fixed payments and taxes consistently higher than 10%;
FCF margin turning consistently positive;
Considerable improvement of corporate governance practices;
Simplification of the Group’s structure;
Better liquidity.
A negative rating action may be prompted by:
Weighted average ratio of FFO before fixed payments to fixed payments falling below 1.0x or weighted average ratio of FFO before net interest payments to interest payments falling below 1.0x;
Using the Company’s cash flows to service the Group’s debt;
FFO margin before fixed payments and taxes falling below 5%.
RATING COMPONENTS
Standalone creditworthiness assessment (SCA): b.
Support: none.
ISSUE RATINGS
No outstanding issues have been rated.
REGULATORY DISCLOSURE
The credit rating has been assigned to Istrinskaya Syrovarnya LLC under the national scale for the Russian Federation based on the Methodology for Assigning Credit Ratings to Non-Financial Corporations under the National Scale for the Russian Federation and the Key Concepts Used by the Analytical Credit Rating Agency within the Scope of Its Rating Activities.
The credit rating of Istrinskaya Syrovarnya LLC was published by ACRA for the first time on June 1, 2023. The credit rating and its outlook are expected to be revised within one year following the publication date of this press release.
The credit rating was assigned based on data provided by Istrinskaya Syrovarnya LLC, information from publicly available sources, and ACRA’s own databases. The credit rating is solicited and Istrinskaya Syrovarnya LLC participated in its assignment.
In assigning the credit rating, ACRA used only information, the quality and reliability of which were, in ACRA’s opinion, appropriate and sufficient to apply the methodologies.
ACRA provided no additional services to Istrinskaya Syrovarnya LLC. No conflicts of interest were discovered in the course of credit rating assignment.

The credit rating of Istrinskaya Syrovarnya LLC (hereinafter, Istrinskaya Syrovarnya, or the Company) is based on a weak assessment of the operational risk profile. The Company is part of the informal Russian Parmesan group (hereinafter, the Group), within which production and trading assets belong to different owners. The assessment of the operational risk profile was also influenced by steady demand for the Company’s products coupled with concentration of its activities on a narrow segment, and a relatively new brand that is only just forming a group of loyal consumers.
The assessment of financial risk profile factors are determined by moderately high leverage amid a very low assessment of business size and high profitability. ACRA assesses the Company’s liquidity as medium and its cash flow as weak.
Russian Parmesan is a Russian producer of cheeses located in the Moscow Region’s Istra district. It produces products under the Oleg Sirota Istrinskaya Syrovarnya brand. Istrinskaya Syrovarnya sells the Group’s products. As of May 2023, the Company had 154 stores in Moscow, the Moscow Region, Saint Petersburg, and the Leningrad Region.
KEY ASSESSMENT FACTORS
Weak operational risk profile assessment. Istrinskaya Syrovarnya is a highly specialized retail chain that sells the products of a single producer. The Company’s brand is rather young and currently in the process of forming a group of loyal consumers. Taking into account the fact that ACRA has analyzed the Group’s retail unit, the assessment of Istrinskaya Syrovarnya’s market position was performed by way of comparison to other retail chains, including those that operate on a federal level. Due to this, the Company’s market position is assessed as low. At the same time, the Agency positively assesses the stability of demand for the Group’s products, which allows Istrinskaya Syrovarnya to increase sales volumes. The Agency assesses the Company’s geography of business as medium because the Company operates a regional chain, but it is concentrated on Moscow (ACRA rating AAA(RU), outlook Stable) and the Moscow Region (ACRA rating AA+(RU), outlook Positive). There are also stores in Saint-Petersburg (ACRA rating AAA(RU), outlook Stable) and the Leningrad Region (ACRA rating AA+(RU), outlook Stable).
ACRA assesses Istrinskaya Syrovarnya’s corporate governance as appropriate for the scale of its activities. The Company does not have a board of directors and corporate procedures are poorly regulated. According to the Agency’s assessments, the Company’s financial transparency is low — the Company only puts together RAS reporting (the auditor is FinEkspertiza LLC). Heightened, in the Agency’s opinion, risks of the structure of the Group have a significant negative impact on the assessment of the factor. Istrinskaya Syrovarnya is part of an informal group. As part of the current structure of the Group, production assets belong to a single owner, while the main owner of the Company is a different entity. At the same time, Istrinskaya Syrovarnya acts as the guarantor for the Group’s bank loans, while the Group only guarantees a small share of the Company’s debt obligations. The owner of production assets has issued a public irrevocable offer with respect to bonds issued by the Company, which, in ACRA's opinion, may mitigate the abovementioned risks.
Very low assessment of business size and high assessment of profitability. Amid the rapid growth of the chain, Istrinskaya Syrovarnya’s revenues increased considerably in 2022 and reached RUB 1.2 bln (vs. RUB 440 mln in 2021). According to the Company’s current strategic plans, the chain is expected to expand from the current 154 stores to 300 by the end of 2025, which may result in revenues gradually growing to RUB 3 bln by late 2025. FFO before fixed payments and taxes grew to RUB 108 mln in 2022, and the Agency expects it to increase to RUB 300 mln by 2025. The size of this indicator, as well as revenues, correspond to a very low assessment of business size as per the Agency’s methodology. The scenario included in ACRA’s forecast does not imply faster chain growth rates. The Agency plans to update its forecast of financial indicators and if necessary adjust the rating if the Company decides to follow a more aggressive strategy.
ACRA rather highly assesses the profitability of Istrinskaya Syrovarnya. Although the FFO margin before fixed payments and taxes is medium (9% as of the end of 2022), EBITDAR per square meter is, according to the Agency’s calculations, RUB 61,000, which corresponds to a very high score and is mainly determined by the retail format. ACRA assumes that profitability indicators will remain at this level in 2023–2025.
Istrinskaya Syrovarnya acts as the guarantor for the Group’s other debt obligations. Taking into account the consolidated statements received from the Company, ACRA assessed the financial standing of the Group as sufficient to independently service its debt without using the cash flows of Istrinskaya Syrovarnya, and therefore this debt was not included in the Company’s leverage. The Agency notes that the above consolidated statements have not been audited.
Moderately high leverage and low interest payment coverage. Istrinskaya Syrovarnya’s debt portfolio consists of a bond issue and loans from JSC Russian Agricultural Bank (ACRA rating AA(RU), outlook Stable). The ratio of the Company’s total debt to FFO before net interest payments in 2022 did not exceed 0.9x, which corresponds to a very low level. However, according to ACRA’s forecast, taking into account bonds placed in 2023, this indicator may reach 3.4x, which is assessed as medium as per the Agency’s methodology. The lease-adjusted debt ratio (lease-adjusted total debt to FFO before fixed payments) in 2022 was high at 4.9x, and may reach 5.96x by the end of 2023. Amid the absence of new borrowings, debt metrics may begin to decline starting from 2024.
Coverage ratios have dynamics that correspond to leverage. According to ACRA’s forecast, in 2023, the ratio of FFO before net interest payments to interest payments may amount to 1.8x, while FFO before fixed payments to fixed payments may stand at 1.2x, which indicates lower coverage of interest and lease payments.
Medium assessment of liquidity and weak cash flow. The free cash flow (FCF) of Istrinskaya Syrovarnya was around zero in 2020–2022. Dividends were not paid. Significant capital expenditures to develop the chain in 2023 may lead to negative FCF during this period, which will be financed using the bond placed this March. Despite the lack of significant repayments in upcoming years, ACRA assesses the Company’s liquidity as medium due to the lack of available credit lines, which may have a negative impact on the liquidity profile in the medium term.
KEY ASSUMPTIONS
Maintaining the current Group structure;
Expansion of the chain to 300 retail points by 2025 while maintaining the current level of profitability;
Maintaining the current product range and supply structure;
No annual dividend payments at the investment stage;
The Group servicing its own debt obligations without using the Company’s cash flows;
Access to external liquidity sources.
POTENTIAL OUTLOOK OR RATING CHANGE FACTORS
The Stable outlook assumes that the rating will highly likely stay unchanged within the 12 to 18-month horizon.
A positive rating action may be prompted by:
Weighted average ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments falling below 2.0x or adjusted total debt to FFO before fixed payments falling below 4.0x;
Weighted average ratio of FFO before fixed payments to fixed payments exceeding 1.5x;
Weighted average FFO before fixed payments and taxes exceeding RUB 500mln or revenues exceeding RUB 5 bln;
FCF margin turning positive;
Considerable improvement of corporate governance practices;
Simplification of the Group’s structure;
Better liquidity.
A negative rating action may be prompted by:
Weighted average ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments exceeding 3.5x or adjusted total debt to FFO before fixed payments exceeding 6.0x;
Weighted average ratio of FFO before fixed payments to fixed payments falling below 1.0x;
Using the Company’s cash flows to service or repay the Group’s debt;
FFO margin before fixed payments and taxes falling below 5%.
RATING COMPONENTS
Standalone creditworthiness assessment (SCA): bb-.
Support: none.
ISSUE RATINGS
No outstanding issues have been rated.
REGULATORY DISCLOSURE
The credit rating has been assigned to Istrinskaya Syrovarnya LLC under the national scale for the Russian Federation based on the Methodology for Credit Ratings Assignment to Non-Financial Corporations under the National Scale for the Russian Federation and the Key Concepts Used by the Analytical Credit Rating Agency within the Scope of Its Rating Activities.
A credit rating has been assigned to Istrinskaya Syrovarnya LLC for the first time. The credit rating and its outlook are expected to be revised within one year following the publication date of this press release.
The credit rating was assigned based on data provided by Istrinskaya Syrovarnya LLC, information from publicly available sources, and ACRA’s own databases. The credit rating is solicited and Istrinskaya Syrovarnya LLC participated in its assignment.
In assigning the credit rating, ACRA used only information, the quality and reliability of which were, in ACRA’s opinion, appropriate and sufficient to apply the methodologies.
ACRA provided no additional services to Istrinskaya Syrovarnya LLC. No conflicts of interest were discovered in the course of credit rating assignment.

The credit rating of DME Limited (hereinafter, the Company, Airport, or Domodedovo) stems from low industry risks coupled with the leading market position of the Company, its strong business profile and strong liquidity as well as high profitability. The credit rating outlook has been changed due on lower coverage of interest payments caused by revaluation of currency denominated liabilities amid shrinking passenger flow. The credit rating is still under pressure from medium leverage and medium quality of corporate governance.
DME Limited is a group of companies that owns and operates the Domodedovo airport. The Airport is the second largest in Russia in terms of passenger and cargo traffic. Dmitry Kamenshchik is the ultimate controlling beneficiary of the Company.
KEY RATING ASSESSMENT FACTORS
Lower coverage of interest payments; leverage remains stable. According to ACRA estimates, at the end of 2017, Domodedovo’s debt amounted to RUB 50 billion and included foreign currency loans and borrowings, as well as amounts due under concession agreements. At the beginning of 2018, the Company issued Eurobonds for USD 300 million in order to refinance another issue of Eurobonds due in November 2018. Coupled with the currency revaluation, this resulted an increase in that the Domodedovo’s liabilities to RUB 73 billion by June 30, 2018. According to Agency estimates, by the end of 2018, the Company's liabilities after the redemption of Eurobonds will amount to RUB 59 billion. Foreign exchange revenues coming from international flights creates a certain natural hedge of currency risks. ACRA estimates the leverage as medium, since the ratio of total debt to FFO before net interest payments will be in the range of 3.6x–3.8x by the end of 2018. Interest coverage has declined, but it is still acceptable. According to ACRA estimates, in 2018, the ratio of FFO before interest payments to interest payments will be 4.1x versus 5.2x at the end of 2017. This is explained, among other things, by the additional interest expenses on the new issue of Eurobonds, which was placed much earlier than the due date of the issue to be refinanced. In the period from 2019 to 2020, we expect a gradual increase in the ratio up to 5.1x.
Strong liquidity and weak free cash flow. As of June 30, 2017, Domodedovo had sufficient liquidity to successfully redeem Eurobonds in November 2018. At the same time, the Company's operations continue to generate a weak free cash flow amid high capital expenditures, which puts pressure on its liquidity. In addition, the volume of dividends declared by the Company in 2018 (RUB 6 billion) is higher than that expected by the Agency (RUB 4 billion). Actual dividend payouts of the Company amounted to RUB 1.4 billion in 1H2018. Thus, according to ACRA estimates for 2018, the FCF margin will be -24%, and the ratio of capital expenditures to revenues will amount to 0.38x. In the period from 2019 to 2020, after the Company has passed the peak of capital expenditures, we expect an increase in free cash flow, but it may still remain negative. Domodedovo’s liquidity is further supported by the availability of open and committed credit facilities in creditor banks. At the same time, we note that the Domodedovo’s leverage exceeded financial covenants for certain obligations, which could lead to certain deterioration in the liquidity position. As a result, by the end of 2018, the short-term liquidity ratio is projected by ACRA at 1.4x. Domodedovo has no large debt obligations to be repaid in the period from 2019 to 2020, and the only negative factor is negative cash flow. Therefore, we expect that, in the mentioned period, the ratio will fluctuate within 1.2x–1.3x.
Low industry risks; leading market positions. Infrastructure companies in the transportation industry have high entry barriers for new players. Oftentimes, airports are natural monopolies that do not compete with each other. There are three airports in the Moscow Air Hub serving the bulk of passenger and cargo traffic in the Russian air transportation market. Domodedovo is the second largest airport in the Moscow Air Hub, being only slightly behind the leader. At the same time, the Agency notes that in January–September 2018, Domodedovo passenger traffic and cargo traffic fell by 3.5% and 4.5%, respectively, which is not in line with the general trend seen in the Moscow Air Hub (+8.6% and +4.3%, respectively).
KEY ASSUMPTIONS
The first Eurobond issue will be refinanced successfully in 2018;
FFO margin before interest payments and taxes will stay within the range of 35%-45%;
The trend for liberalization of the state tariff policy with respect to airport infrastructure will remain;
Dividend payouts will not exceed RUB 4 bln;
Passenger traffic will grow by 2%-4% annually;
Capital expenditures will remain high (30%-35% of revenues). 
POTENTIAL OUTLOOK OR RATING CHANGE FACTORS
The Negative outlook assumes that the rating may be downgraded within the 12 to 18-month horizon.
A positive rating action may be prompted by:
A decline of total debt to FFO before interest payments below 2.0x or increase of FFO before net interest payments to interest payments above 10x;
An increase of FCF margin after dividends above 2%;
A substantial cut of the capex program (below 15% of revenues);
A significant improvement in corporate governance practices, including the board of directors and the internal regulatory framework.
A negative rating action may be prompted by:
Continued annual dividend payouts exceeding RUB 5 bln amid negative FCF;
Domestic and international passenger traffic declining by more than 5%-10% in 2019-2020 vs 2018;
Total debt to FFO before net interest payments to interest payments exceeding 4.0x or FFO before net interest payments to net interest payments dropping below 5.0x;
A loss of a large client, which may result in a financial performance decline of the Company;
FFO margin before net interest payments and taxes decreasing falling below 25%;
A substantially worse access to external sources of liquidity;
A change in the state tariff policy for airport infrastructure.
RATING COMPONENTS
Standalone creditworthiness assessment (SCA): a+.
Adjustments: none.
ISSUE RATINGS
Bond loan, Exchange-traded interest-bearing certified bearer bonds issued by Domodedovo Fuel Facilities Ltd (ISIN RU000A0ZYM21), maturity date: December 20, 2022, issue volume: RUB 10 bln, – A+(RU).
Credit rating rationale. The Bond issue is a senior unsecured debt obligation of Domodedovo Fuel Facilities Ltd. (hereinafter, the Issuer), which is an operational subsidiary of DME Limited. The credit rating was assigned on the basis of public irrevocable offers from DME Limited as a holding company and from key operational companies of the holding company generating the bulk of the Group’s cash flow. The list of offerers is identical to the list of companies acting as sureties under Eurobonds issued by the Group, which, in ACRA’s opinion, is indicative of no structural subordination of the issues. Therefore, and in view of no contractual subordination, ACRA ranks the bond pari passu with other existing and future unsecured and unsubordinated obligations of the Company. At the same time, ACRA notes the lack of a suretyship from the owner of Domodedovo’s assets – Hacienda Limited (Cyprus) – with respect to both the rated bond issue and Eurobonds issued earlier. In view of the above, ACRA used a detailed approach to assess loss recovery rate. According to ACRA’s methodology, the recovery rate for Domodedovo’s unsecured debt is classified into the second category, and therefore, the credit rating of the issue is on par with the credit rating of DME Limited — A+(RU).
REGULATORY DISCLOSURE
The credit ratings of DME Limited and the bond (ISIN RU000A0ZYM21) issued by Domodedovo Fuel Facilities Ltd., a subsidiary of DME Limited, were assigned under the national scale for the Russian Federation based on the Methodology for Credit Ratings Assignment to Non-Financial Corporations Under the National Scale for the Russian Federation and the Key Concepts Used by the Analytical Credit Rating Agency within the Scope of Its Rating Activities. The Methodology for Assigning Credit Ratings to Individual Issues of Financial Instruments Under the National Scale of the Russian Federation was also used in the credit rating assignment of the specified issue.
The credit ratings of DME Limited and the bond (ISIN RU000A0ZYM21) issued by Domodedovo Fuel Facilities Ltd. were first published by ACRA on November 29, 2017 and December 26, 2017, respectively. The credit rating and outlook of DME Limited and the credit rating of the bond (ISIN RU000A0ZYM21) issued by Domodedovo Fuel Facilities Ltd. are expected to be revised within one year following the rating action date (November 22, 2018).
Disclosure of deviations from approved methodologies: the profitability factor was assessed with a deviation from the assessment range as specified in the methodology as a result of a comparison with a group of comparable companies.
The assigned credit rating is based on the data provided by DME Limited, information from publicly available sources, as well as ACRA’s own databases. The credit rating is solicited, and DME Limited participated in its assignment.
No material discrepancies between the provided data and the data officially disclosed by DME Limited in its financial statements have been discovered.
ACRA provided no additional services to DME Limited. No conflicts of interest were discovered in the course of credit rating assignment.

The credit rating assigned to DME Limited (hereinafter, the Company, Airport, or Domodedovo) stems from low industry risks coupled with the leading market position of the Company, its strong business profile and strong liquidity as well as high profitability. On the other hand, the credit rating is under pressure from medium leverage and medium level of corporate governance.
DME Limited is a group of companies that own and operate infrastructure of the Domodedovo airport. The Airport is the second largest in Russia in terms of passenger and cargo traffic. Dmitry Kamenshchik is the ultimate controlling beneficiary of the Company.
KEY RATING ASSESSMENT FACTORS
Low industry risks for DME Limited coupled with a leading market position. Infrastructure companies in the transportation industry have high entry barriers for new players. More often than not, airports represent natural monopolies, which predetermines lack of any direct competition between them. There are three airports in the Moscow Air Hub with the major passenger and cargo traffic in the Russian air transportation market. Domodedovo is the second largest airport in the Moscow Air Hub being only slightly behind the leader.
Strong business profile. Airport’s infrastructure is well developed: it includes two independent parallel runways and passenger and cargo terminals (256,000 sq. m and 13,400 sq. m, respectively). Fuel installations are capable of refilling 10 aircrafts per hour. By commissioning a new runway and gradual commissioning of new premises in the terminal, Airport’s capacity may increase after 2018. ACRA assesses the investment program implemented by the Company as large-scale with low flexibility. Domodedovo services passenger and cargo traffic of Moscow and Moscow Region that exhibit high social and economic development. Having a status of federal international airport the Company may service international flights of Russian airlines as well as accommodate departure and arrival flights of international airlines.
Medium leverage and high coverage. According to ACRA estimates, the debt of Domodedovo amounted to RUB 41.3 bln as at year-end 2016 comprising foreign currency-denominated loans and borrowings as well as debt under concession agreements. ACRA notes a substantial amount of the Company’s foreign currency commitments. Foreign currency revenue from servicing international flights represents somewhat of a natural hedge of FX risks. ACRA assesses leverage as medium as total debt to FFO before net interest payments ratio would stand at 3.6x by year-end 2017. Coverage is high: FFO before interest payments to interest payments would be 5.7x in 2017, according to ACRA estimates. We expect no significant changes in the leverage and coverage indicators of the Company in 2018-2019.
Medium size and high profitability. Following 2016 results, revenues of Domodedovo were at RUB 38.2 bln, and FFO before net interest payments and taxes stood at RUB 14.3 bln, which corresponds to a medium business size (according to ACRA classification). FFO margin before net interest payments and taxes was 39%. The Airport serviced 28.5 mln passengers, and the cargo traffic was 142,200 tons. In view of a significant increase in passenger traffic in 2017, ACRA expects revenues and FFO before net interest payments and taxes to grow by 9%. We expect the 2017 profitability to stand at 37%. ACRA expects no significant increase in the Company’s revenue and FFO before net interest payments and taxes.
Strong liquidity and weak cash flow. Debt obligations of Domodedovo are of long-term nature. The only large payment in the forecast period (2017-2019) is due in 2018 when the Company has to repay the first Eurobonds issue (USD 220 mln). This fact exerts pressure on the liquidity. We expect short-term liquidity ratio to be at 1.3x in 2018-2019. Another factor restraining liquidity is a massive investment program (we estimate the average capital expenditures to revenues ratio to be around 35% in 2017-2018), with the substantial spending involved driving free cash flow (FCF) into a negative territory. According to ACRA forecast, FCF margin of Domodedovo would be negative in 2017-2018 (around -15%), and in 2019 and thereafter, it would approach zero.
Medium level of corporate governance. Strategic plans of Domodedovo are primarily related to the Airport’s capacity increase and transport infrastructure development as well as operational efficiency improvement. In ACRA’s opinion, the strategy is not sufficiently formalized. The Company’s Board of Directors does not have enough rights, and management makes key decisions in view of the major shareholder’s interests. The bulk of Domodedovo’s property is on the books of a subsidiary registered in Cyprus. The group’s structure is complicated as various businesses are managed by separate legal entities, which, however, is economically justified. The Company has an established risk management system, the Agency, however, is concerned by the practice of making large dividend payouts while FCF is negative. Financial information disclosure is high enough.
KEY ASSUMPTIONS
Successful refinancing of the first Eurobonds issue in 2018;
FFO margin before interest payments and taxes stays within the range of 35%-45%;
The trend is preserved for liberalization of the state tariff policy with respect to airport infrastructure;
Dividend payouts do not exceed RUB 4 bln;
2%-4% of annual passenger traffic growth;
Substantial expenses are maintained to finance capital expenditures (of 30%-35% of revenues). 
POTENTIAL OUTLOOK OR RATING CHANGE FACTORS
The Stable outlook assumes that the rating will most likely stay unchanged within the 12 to 18-month horizon.
A positive rating action may be prompted by:
A decline of total debt to FFO before interest payments below 2.0x or increase of FFO before net interest payments to interest payments above 10x;
An increase of FCF margin after dividends above 2%;
A substantial cut of the capex program (below 15% of revenues);
A significant improvement in corporate governance practices as to the development of the board of directors as a management body as well as of the regulatory base governing its activities.
A negative rating action may be prompted by:
Annual dividend payouts exceeding RUB 5 bln;
Domestic and international passenger traffic declining by more than 10%-15% in 2018-2019 vs 2017;
Total debt to FFO before net interest payments to interest payments exceeding 4.0x or FFO before net interest payments to net interest payments dropping below 5.0x;
A loss of a large client, which may result in a financial performance decline of the Company;
FFO margin before net interest payments and taxes decreasing falling below 25%;
A substantially worse access to external sources of liquidity;
A change in the state tariff policy for airport infrastructure.
RATING COMPONENTS
Standalone creditworthiness assessment (SCA): a+.
Adjustments: none.
ISSUE RATINGS
No outstanding issues have been rated.
REGULATORY DISCLOSURE
The credit rating has been assigned under the national scale for the Russian Federation and is based on the Methodology for Credit Ratings Assignment to Non-Financial Corporations under the National Scale for the Russian Federation, and the Key Concepts Used by the Analytical Credit Rating Agency within the Scope of Its Rating Activities.
A credit rating has been assigned to DME Limited for the first time. The credit rating and its outlook are expected to be revised within one year following the rating action date (November 27, 2017).
Disclosure of deviations from approved methodologies: the profitability factor was assessed with a deviation from the assessment range as specified in the methodology.
The assigned credit rating is based on the data provided by DME Limited, information from publicly available sources, as well as ACRA’s own databases. The credit rating is solicited, and DME Limited participated in its assignment.
No material discrepancies between the provided data and the data officially disclosed by DME Limited in its financial statements have been discovered.
ACRA provided no additional services to DME Limited. No conflicts of interest were discovered in the course of credit rating assignment.

The credit rating of JSC MaximaTelecom (hereinafter, MaximaTelecom, the Company, or the Group) has been downgraded due a combination of factors, including weaker results in 2021 than ACRA anticipated, as well as lower FFO before fixed payments and taxes compared to the Agency’s expectations set out in the 2022 forecast model. The Negative outlook has been retained to reflect the Agency’s concerns about the approaching put option date (in H2 2023) of one of the bonds issued by the Group.
In other respects, MaximaTelecom’s rating is a combination of weak cash flow, small company size, strong business profile and market position, low profitability, medium liquidity, and the Company’s presence in economically developed regions.
MaximaTelecom develops digital solutions and carries out infrastructure projects for businesses and the state in key economic sectors such as transport, industrial production, urban services, housing and utilities, and other areas. The Company carries out major projects involving the creation of ICT infrastructure, systems integration, and develops digital products for smart cities and the digitalization of production based on data analysis, artificial intelligence, machine vision, video analysis, wireless communications, the Internet of Things, and additional reality, including Industria 4.0 tools. The Company deploys infrastructure and platform solutions in the metro systems of Moscow and Saint Petersburg, urban transportation systems, the rolling stock of leading Russian producers, depos and situation centers, and at production assets. MaximaTelecom is one of Russia’s systemically important IT enterprises.
KEY ASSESSMENT FACTORS
Very high leverage. The Company’s debt is mainly made up of two fixed-rate bond issues worth a total of RUB 5.5 bln. As of the end of 2021, the ratio of adjusted total debt to FFO before fixed payments, according to ACRA’s predictions, exceeded 5.0x (the boundary of the current factor assessment category) and reached 9.8x, while the ratio of FFO before fixed payments to fixed payments was 0.98x, which together indicate very high leverage. In view of the current contract base, ACRA assumes that these indicators may improve to 5.0–7.0x and 1.5–2.0x, respectively, in 2022. Further performance of MaximaTelecom’s leverage indicators will depend on the level of contracting and the profitability of these contracts. ACRA will closely monitor the Company’s contract base.
Medium liquidity and weak cash flow assessment. In H2 2023, MaximaTelecom will have a put option for the first bond issue amounting to RUB 3 bln. The current liquidity assessment continues to assume a proactive search for sources of liquidity required for the successful completion of the put option. ACRA is closely monitoring the Company’s liquidity profile and, in the event of a deviation from the indicated prerequisites, may adjust the assessment of the factor, which may lead to a rating revision. In 2021, free cash flow (FCF) was negative (the FCF margin was -20% on the back of dividend payments and excluding RUB 2.1 bln generated by the sale of a stake in MT-Technologies LLC). According to ACRA’s forecast, maintaining a negative value of the indicator in 2022–2023 may put pressure on the liquidity assessment.
Low assessment of size of business and low profitability. The Company’s revenues in 2021 amounted to RUB 8.8 bln, which is generally in line with ACRA’s forecast. Taking into account the current portfolio of contracts, MaximaTelecom’s revenues may increase substantially and reach RUB 18–20 bln in 2022. Most of this amount has already been secured by concluded contracts. MaximaTelecom’s profitability in 2021 was below ACRA’s projection — FFO profitability before fixed payments and taxes was 8%, while FFO before fixed payments and taxes amounted to RUB 681 mln, which corresponds to a low assessment of the Company’s size. According to the Agency’s estimates, in 2022 profitability may remain at a level similar to that of 2021. In 2023–2024, the performance of these indicators will depend on the budgets allocated by the main customers for digitalization of urban infrastructure, transport systems, and new business lines associated with digitalization of industrial sectors, and the profitability of these projects.
Strong business profile. Since 2020, the Company’s revenue structure has been dominated by digitalization projects in the urban environment, transport sector, and rolling stock. In the Agency’s opinion, the experience gained by the Company in implementing comprehensive automation projects in various urban services, production processes, and transport infrastructure, and the implementation of platform solutions at Russian enterprises may become an additional competitive advantage in developing its portfolio of projects. In the coming years, large cities, industrial enterprises and the transport sector will continue to invest in the digitalization of infrastructure and thereby increase efficiency through digital transformation, and this will have a positive effect on the prospects of this market. However, possible difficulties related to the acquisition of foreign telecoms equipment may influence these processes. The Company’s operations are focused on regions favorable for its business — the bulk of its proceeds comes from projects implemented in Moscow (ACRA rating AAA(RU), outlook Stable) and Saint Petersburg (ACRA rating AAA(RU), outlook Stable).
KEY ASSUMPTIONS
Revenue within RUB 18–20 bln in 2022;
Ratio of adjusted total debt to FFO before fixed payments within 5.0–6.0x;
FFO profitability before fixed payments and taxes within 5–7% this year;
Possibility of acquiring the necessary equipment for the Company to fulfill its contractual obligations;
Early refinancing of peak repayments in 2023.
POTENTIAL OUTLOOK OR RATING CHANGE FACTORS
The Negative outlook assumes that the rating will highly likely be downgraded within the 12 to 18-month horizon.
A positive rating action may be prompted by:
Weighted average ratio of adjusted total debt to FFO before fixed payments falling below 5.0x;
Weighted average ratio of FFO before fixed payments to fixed payments exceeding 3.0x;
Weighted average FFO before fixed payments and taxes exceeding RUB 5 bln;
Weighted FFO profitability before fixed payments and taxes exceeding 20%;
Positive weighted FCF profitability.
A negative rating action may be prompted by:
Weighted average ratio of FFO before fixed payments to fixed payments falling below 1.0x;
FFO profitability before fixed payments and taxes remaining below 10%;
Continued practice of dividend payouts amid high leverage;
Revenue falling below RUB 8 bln;
Considerable difficulties in acquiring equipment;
Downgrade in quantitative or qualitative assessments of liquidity.
RATING COMPONENTS
Standalone creditworthiness assessment (SCA): bb+.
Support: none.
ISSUE RATINGS
Bond issue JSC MaximaTelecom (RU000A101XD8), maturity date: July 14, 2026, put option date: July 18, 2023, issue volume: RUB 3 bln — BB+(RU).
Bond issue JSC MaximaTelecom (RU000A102DK3), maturity date: May 21, 2024, call option date: November 23, 2021, issue volume: RUB 2.5 bln — BB+(RU).
Rationale. The issues represent senior unsecured debt of MaximaTelecom. Due to the absence of either structural or contractual subordination of the issues, ACRA regards them as equal to other existing and future unsecured and unsubordinated debt obligations of the Company in terms of priority. ACRA applied the detailed approach to assess the level of compensation for losses. In accordance with this approach and the Agency’s methodology, the level of recovery for the issues is category II, and due to this the credit ratings of the issues are equivalent to that of the Company, i.e. BB+(RU).
REGULATORY DISCLOSURE
The credit ratings have been assigned to JSC MaximaTelecom and the bonds (RU000A101XD8, RU000A102DK3) issued by JSC MaximaTelecom under the national scale for the Russian Federation based on the Methodology for Credit Ratings Assignment to Non-Financial Corporations under the National Scale for the Russian Federation and Key Concepts Used by the Analytical Credit Rating Agency within the Scope of Its Rating Activities. The Methodology for Assigning Credit Ratings to Individual Issues of Financial Instruments on the National Scale of the Russian Federation was also applied to assign the credit ratings to the bond issues listed above.
The credit ratings of JSC MaximaTelecom and the bonds issued by JSC MaximaTelecom (RU000A101XD8, RU000A102DK3) were assigned by ACRA for the first time on February 26, 2020, July 21, 2020, and November 24, 2020, respectively. The credit rating of JSC MaximaTelecom and its outlook and the credit ratings of the bonds issued by JSC MaximaTelecom (RU000A101XD8, RU000A102DK3) are expected to be revised within one year following the publication date of this press release.
The credit ratings were assigned based on data provided by JSC MaximaTelecom, information from publicly available sources, and ACRA’s own databases. The credit ratings are solicited, and JSC MaximaTelecom participated in their assignment.
In assigning the credit ratings, ACRA used only information, the quality and reliability of which was, in ACRA’s opinion, appropriate and sufficient to apply the methodologies.
ACRA provided no additional services to JSC MaximaTelecom. No conflicts of interest were identified in the course of credit rating assignment.

ACRA has affirmed the following ratings to the Government of the Russian Federation (hereinafter, Russia, or the country) under the international scale:
Long-term foreign currency credit rating at A- and local currency credit rating at A-;
Short-term foreign currency rating at S1 and local currency credit rating at S1.
The outlook on the long-term foreign currency credit rating is Stable and local currency credit rating is Stable.
The Stable outlook assumes that the rating will most likely stay unchanged within the 12 to 18-month horizon.
The Stable outlook indicates that the government of the Russian Federation has a wide range of opportunities for counter-cyclical fiscal policy in light of the expected cyclical slowdown in economic growth in 2020 due to an adverse external environment and the coronavirus pandemic. Debt financing the deficit is not necessary and is likely to be used very sparingly given the significant amount of liquid balances in the consolidated budget. The coordination of economic policies and their long-term nature, which significantly reduced the dependence of the national currency exchange rate on fluctuations in world prices for Russian export goods, pave the way for a moderate increase in inflation in 2020 and a relatively restrained reaction in consumption, investments, and household income. Russia’s stable external position is supported by the fact that international reserves exceed the external debt of the government and other sectors (by 15% as of January 1, 2020), with the external debt itself being partially denominated (28.5%) in national currency. This amount of reserves is more than sufficient both to compensate for excessive exchange rate volatility and to refinance the short-term foreign exchange debt of Russian companies. ACRA is monitoring the situation on a daily basis. If new and significant information becomes available on the effects of the spread of coronavirus or on the state of the external environment, ACRA may review the credit ratings of the Russian Federation and/or credit rating outlook earlier than the date set in the Calendar of planned sovereign credit rating revisions and publications.
CREDIT RATING RATIONALE
Russia’s A- sovereign credit rating is supported by the low level and sustainable structure of public debt, strong external position, and an effective monetary policy coordinated with fiscal policy that significantly improves macroeconomic stability. The rating is constrained by the low potential for economic growth, limited diversity of exports, lack of transparency of the Russian Government’s obligations to a number of state-owned companies, weakness of institutions, and the threat of new sanctions capable of limiting investment and hindering technology-based cooperation and trade with potential foreign partners.
SOVEREIGN MODEL APPLICATION RESULTS
Russia has been assigned an A Indicative credit rating in accordance with the core part of ACRA’s sovereign model. A number of modifiers in the modifiers part of the model allow the Indicative credit rating to be increased. These include the following, which are determined by the Methodology for Credit Rating Assignment to Sovereign Entities under the International Scale:
Efficacy of structural, economic and monetary policies;
Fiscal policy framework and fiscal flexibility;
Debt sustainability;
External debt sustainability.
Negative modifiers include the following:
Potential economic growth;
Sustainability of economic growth;
Contingent liabilities and the risk of them materializing on the sovereign’s balance sheet;
Balance of payment vulnerabilities;
Exposure to geopolitical risks.
The Assigned credit rating of A- is one notch lower than the Indicative credit rating as the total effect of negative adjustments exceeds the total effect of positive adjustments. There are no extraordinary factors that could adjust the Final credit rating.
POTENTIAL RATING UPGRADE FACTORS
Increased economic growth while maintaining macroeconomic stability;
Diversification of exported goods;
Increased transparency between the Russian Government and state-owned companies;
Growth rates of human capital quality (health, education, etc.) outpacing those of developed countries.
POTENTIAL RATING DOWNGRADE FACTORS
Significant easing or abandonment of the fiscal rule for the federal budget;
Decrease in the actual liquidity of international reserves (for example, as a result of increased credit or operational risks);
Significant increase in short-term public debt.
ISSUE RATINGS
No outstanding issues have been rated.
REGULATORY DISCLOSURE
The sovereign credit ratings have been assigned to the Russian Federation under the international scale based on the Methodology for Credit Rating Assignment to Sovereign Entities under the International Scale and the Key Concepts Used by the Analytical Credit Rating Agency Within the Scope of Its Rating Activities.
The sovereign credit ratings of the Russian Federation were published for the first time on September 23, 2019. The sovereign credit ratings and their outlook are expected to be revised within 182 days following the publication date of this press release as per the Calendar of planned sovereign credit rating revisions and publications.
The sovereign credit ratings are based on information from publicly available sources, as well as ACRA’s own databases. The sovereign credit ratings are unsolicited. The Government of the Russian Federation participated in the credit rating assignment.
ACRA provided no additional services to the Government of the Russian Federation. No conflicts of interest were discovered in the course of the sovereign credit rating assignment.

